WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.448 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.448 --> 00:00:03.909 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:09.885 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:09.885 --> 00:00:12.260 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today, we're
going to start analyzing games.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:19.652 align:middle line:90%


00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.360 align:middle line:84%
So in my view, this
is where things start

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.760 align:middle line:90%
getting a lot more interesting.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.740 align:middle line:84%
So far, we've just said how
to set up a game-- or first,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.980 align:middle line:84%
how to set up an individual
decision problem,

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.937 align:middle line:84%
and then how to set up
or represent a game.

00:00:29.937 --> 00:00:31.520 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're
actually going to start

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.320 align:middle line:84%
thinking about
analyzing the game

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.120 align:middle line:84%
and trying to make
predictions about how

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.400 align:middle line:90%
people will play in the game.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.800 align:middle line:84%
So I want to first
go over the approach

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.880 align:middle line:84%
that we're usually going
to take in this course.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.480 align:middle line:84%
We'll usually start with
some informal description

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.615 align:middle line:90%
of a strategic setting.

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:51.740 align:middle line:84%
That may be something that
you get in a problem set

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.080 align:middle line:84%
or from thinking
about the world.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.740 align:middle line:84%
Then, based on the
informal description,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.560 align:middle line:84%
we'll usually write down the
extensive form representation.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:06.500 align:middle line:90%


00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.060 align:middle line:84%
Remember, this is the very
detailed representation

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.700 align:middle line:84%
that we talked about
a lot on Tuesday.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.180 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll reduce this
extensive form representation

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.580 align:middle line:84%
to the strategic
form representation.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.380 align:middle line:90%


00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.940 align:middle line:84%
And later on in
the course, we will

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.700 align:middle line:84%
discuss some issues
that can only

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.487 align:middle line:84%
be perceived in the extensive
form representation.

00:01:33.487 --> 00:01:35.820 align:middle line:84%
But for the beginning of the
course-- and in particular,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.820 align:middle line:90%
in today--

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.500 align:middle line:84%
everything we're
going to say is going

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.300 align:middle line:84%
to be about a strategic
form representation.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So today, our starting
point is going to be 3.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.740 align:middle line:84%
We're going to imagine that
we're given a strategic form

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.320 align:middle line:90%
game.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.180 align:middle line:84%
But you can imagine,
in the background,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.250 align:middle line:84%
that we may have derived arrived
this given strategic form

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.530 align:middle line:84%
game from an extensive form
that we previously wrote down.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.330 align:middle line:84%
So let's just recall the
notation for a strategic form

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.010 align:middle line:90%
game.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.970 align:middle line:84%
We have the players,
i equals 1 through n

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.770 align:middle line:84%
And then for each player i, we
have a strategy set associated

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.050 align:middle line:90%
with that player i.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.690 align:middle line:84%
So we have big S1
up to big Sn, where

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.310 align:middle line:84%
S1 is the set of strategies
that are available to player 1.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.410 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the set can
be quite complicated,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.850 align:middle line:84%
if we've derived this
set by evaluating

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.690 align:middle line:84%
all complete contingent
plans in the extensive form

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.330 align:middle line:90%
representation.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:42.290 align:middle line:84%
And then we have our payoffs,
ui from S to R, where, remember,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.730 align:middle line:84%
S is the set of
strategy profiles.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:51.410 align:middle line:84%
So S, mathematically, is a
product set of S1 up to Sn.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.890 align:middle line:84%
But the key thing is that
what lives in S are profiles

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.990 align:middle line:84%
files specifying a strategy
for each of the n players.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.490 align:middle line:84%
And we have a
function like this,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.670 align:middle line:84%
again for each of the n players,
that specifies each player i's

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.550 align:middle line:84%
utility as a function of
the profile of strategies

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:09.690 align:middle line:90%
that the players play.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.790 align:middle line:90%


00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:18.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, what is our goal
when we analyze a game?

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.230 align:middle line:84%
I think we're asking the
following fundamental question--

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.030 align:middle line:84%
given a game, we want
to ask, how do we

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.410 align:middle line:84%
think players will play
or should play the game?

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:46.430 align:middle line:90%


00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, notice, I think the
distinction between will play

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.610 align:middle line:84%
and should play gets into
two different interpretations

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.250 align:middle line:90%
of the analysis that we do.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:58.530 align:middle line:84%
So the answers that we get
and the formal analysis we do

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.710 align:middle line:84%
won't really depend on
which interpretation

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:01.870 align:middle line:90%
you want to keep in mind.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.270 align:middle line:84%
But depending on
the application,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.570 align:middle line:84%
one may be more or
less compelling.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.630 align:middle line:84%
So will play-- if we want
to think about will play,

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.290 align:middle line:84%
this is what sometimes
called positive analysis

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:16.130 align:middle line:90%
or descriptive analysis.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.130 align:middle line:84%
And the idea of this is that,
if we observe some game,

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.970 align:middle line:84%
we want to be able to make
a prediction about how

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.730 align:middle line:84%
rational players will
choose to play this game.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:29.810 align:middle line:84%
So the goal here is
really a prediction,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.690 align:middle line:84%
which will, of
course, only be valid

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.450 align:middle line:84%
if we think that the utility
functions that we write down

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.210 align:middle line:84%
and the rationality assumptions
that we make are compelling.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.010 align:middle line:84%
Another interpretation
of game theory

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.770 align:middle line:84%
is to put yourself in
the role of an advisor.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.570 align:middle line:84%
Someone comes to you and says,
how should I play in this game?

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.933 align:middle line:84%
What is your recommendation
for how I should play?

00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:50.850 align:middle line:84%
And companies do sometimes
hire game theorists

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.033 align:middle line:84%
to consult and make
recommendations for maybe

00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:54.450 align:middle line:84%
how they should
bid in an auction.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:55.670 align:middle line:90%
That would be one example.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:05:00.550 align:middle line:84%
So here, this is
sometimes called normative

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:05.710 align:middle line:90%
or prescriptive analysis.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.750 align:middle line:84%
And the goal of our
analysis is to provide

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:17.310 align:middle line:84%
what I might say is a
recommendation for how players

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:22.250 align:middle line:84%
should play if they want
to achieve their objectives

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.050 align:middle line:84%
and they recognize that other
players are rational as well

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.495 align:middle line:84%
and maybe also are getting
advice from game theorists

00:05:27.495 --> 00:05:29.870 align:middle line:84%
or from other informed parties
about how they should play

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:30.910 align:middle line:90%
in the game.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.750 align:middle line:84%
But I just want you to keep
this in mind, in the background,

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:34.830 align:middle line:90%
throughout the course.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.247 align:middle line:84%
But when you're formally asked
to solve for an equilibrium

00:05:37.247 --> 00:05:39.830 align:middle line:84%
or to analyze a
game, your answer

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.510 align:middle line:84%
won't really depend on your
particular interpretation

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:45.550 align:middle line:90%
in that context.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.860 align:middle line:84%
So I want to start with an
example of a game, an example

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.243 align:middle line:90%
of a strategic form game.

00:05:51.243 --> 00:05:53.660 align:middle line:84%
And this is the most famous
game in game theory, probably.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:54.600 align:middle line:90%
You may have heard of it.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.080 align:middle line:84%
It's called the
prisoner's dilemma.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.620 align:middle line:90%


00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.440 align:middle line:90%
Make sure to [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.460 align:middle line:90%


00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.860 align:middle line:84%
Now, the first thing
I want to write down

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.000 align:middle line:84%
is think about how we
can represent this game.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.500 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of ugly to think about
all these functions and all

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.540 align:middle line:90%
these sets.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.700 align:middle line:84%
So normally, we represent
strategic form games

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.020 align:middle line:84%
using what's called a
by matrix, just a box

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.220 align:middle line:90%
with some numbers in it.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.100 align:middle line:84%
So in this game,
each player is going

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.100 align:middle line:84%
to have two strategies,
which we'll call M and F. So

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.520 align:middle line:90%
let me tell the story here.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:32.840 align:middle line:90%
So we have two prisoners.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.620 align:middle line:90%


00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.180 align:middle line:84%
And we assume that they're
placed in separate cells.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.260 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is going
to be a colorful story

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.360 align:middle line:84%
to help motivate
this particular game.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.272 align:middle line:84%
But the lessons
from this game, I

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.480 align:middle line:84%
think a lot of game theorists
and economists believe,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.280 align:middle line:84%
are much broader than
this kind of silly story

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:52.760 align:middle line:90%
that we're going to tell here.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.700 align:middle line:90%
So we have two prisoners.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.640 align:middle line:84%
And they've been
accused of a crime.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.040 align:middle line:84%
And each prisoner
has two options.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.760 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them M and
F. So M is to stay mum--

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.360 align:middle line:84%
that is, don't
admit to anything.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.265 align:middle line:84%
And F is sometimes the word
is used fink or tattle.

00:07:09.265 --> 00:07:11.140 align:middle line:84%
It's to tell on the
other person-- say, yeah,

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.760 align:middle line:90%
the other guy did it.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.640 align:middle line:84%
So the prosecutor
comes by and says,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:19.120 align:middle line:84%
do you want to accuse
your co-conspirator

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.640 align:middle line:90%
of committing the crime?

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.420 align:middle line:84%
And the prosecutor says, look,
we'll give you a good deal.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So if you're willing to testify
against the other prisoner,

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.888 align:middle line:84%
we'll give you a
sentence reduction.

00:07:29.888 --> 00:07:32.180 align:middle line:84%
But now, we're going to be
able to catch the other guy.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.463 align:middle line:84%
And the other guy is going
to get a bigger sentence.

00:07:34.463 --> 00:07:35.880 align:middle line:84%
So now, what we
want to do is fill

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.560 align:middle line:90%
in the payoffs in this matrix.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And what we imagine is, if
both players stay silent, then

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.060 align:middle line:90%
both players get a payoff of 2.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.340 align:middle line:84%
They both get convicted
of, say, a minor crime,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.820 align:middle line:84%
because they didn't
have testimony

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.540 align:middle line:90%
to convict either of them.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:54.460 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's say I'm player 1.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:08:01.860 align:middle line:84%
So the way I'm writing this
here is player 1 is the column--

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.540 align:middle line:90%
sorry, the row player.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.440 align:middle line:90%
Player 1 is choosing the row.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.460 align:middle line:84%
So they're choosing
between M and F. Player 2

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:10.940 align:middle line:90%
is the column player.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.660 align:middle line:84%
They're choosing between
the column m and F.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.500 align:middle line:84%
So the strategy set-- maybe
this is helpful here--

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:22.460 align:middle line:84%
for each player S1 equals
S2 equals M comma F.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:26.180 align:middle line:84%
So if we want to formally
connect it to our strategic form

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:30.080 align:middle line:84%
representation over here, each
player has two strategies.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.800 align:middle line:84%
They each have the
same two strategies,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.179 align:middle line:84%
simply staying silent,
M, or testifying

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:38.260 align:middle line:84%
against the other
player, finking, F.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:41.940 align:middle line:84%
And then when we write 2, 2
in this box, the first number

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.330 align:middle line:84%
represents player 1's utility
from this strategy profile

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.930 align:middle line:90%
corresponding to this box.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:52.050 align:middle line:84%
So this first box corresponds
to the strategy profile where

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.650 align:middle line:84%
player one chooses M
and player 2 chooses M.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:58.890 align:middle line:84%
And then in the box, we're
writing player 1'2 payoff from

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.450 align:middle line:84%
that consequence or
that strategy profile,

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:04.690 align:middle line:84%
and then player 2's payoff
from that strategy profile.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:05.710 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's go down here.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.410 align:middle line:84%
We want to say what
happens if player 1 instead

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.690 align:middle line:84%
testifies or finks against
the other prisoner.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.890 align:middle line:84%
What this should do
is it should increase

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:17.610 align:middle line:84%
the utility of the prisoner who
finks, because that prisoner

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.570 align:middle line:90%
gets a sentence reduction.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.635 align:middle line:84%
But it decreases the utility
of the other prisoner,

00:09:22.635 --> 00:09:24.010 align:middle line:84%
because the other
prisoner is now

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.650 align:middle line:84%
going to be convicted
of a more serious crime.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:29.870 align:middle line:84%
So the exact numbers we'll
use are 3 and negative 1.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:34.690 align:middle line:90%


00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.630 align:middle line:84%
So relative to the case
where they both stay silent,

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:42.430 align:middle line:84%
if I fink on the other prisoner,
my utility goes up by 1 util.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:47.430 align:middle line:84%
But my opponent's utility
goes down by 3 utils.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.390 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going to put the
symmetric payoffs over here.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:54.750 align:middle line:84%
But remember, over here, it's
prisoner 2 who's testifying.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.150 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, prisoner
2 gets the higher payoff.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.150 align:middle line:84%
And prisoner 1 gets
the lower payoff.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:03.923 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're going to put
negative 1, 3 in this box.

00:10:03.923 --> 00:10:05.590 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, we
have to decide what

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:08.430 align:middle line:90%
happens if they both testify.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.070 align:middle line:90%
And here, the payoff is 0, 0.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.510 align:middle line:84%
So the exact numbers
aren't so important.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:15.330 align:middle line:84%
But the order is
pretty important.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.030 align:middle line:90%
So why is it 0 here?

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:21.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, the other person
is testifying against me.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.670 align:middle line:84%
So I get convicted of
a pretty serious crime.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.530 align:middle line:84%
But it's not quite as bad
as the negative 1 over here,

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.990 align:middle line:84%
because I still get a little
bit of a sentence reduction

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:32.510 align:middle line:84%
for testifying
against the other guy.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:34.830 align:middle line:84%
So you can see,
there's an ordering

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.010 align:middle line:90%
between the four outcomes.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:37.510 align:middle line:84%
And this is really
what's important.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.890 align:middle line:90%


00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:46.170 align:middle line:84%
So the highest payoff I get is
if I testify and the other guy

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.290 align:middle line:90%
doesn't.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.590 align:middle line:84%
The worst payoff is if the
other prisoner testifies

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:53.890 align:middle line:84%
and I don't, because
then I get convicted

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.250 align:middle line:84%
and I don't get any
sentence reduction.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.450 align:middle line:84%
And then in between,
we have the payoff

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.650 align:middle line:84%
where we both stay silent,
because we only get charged

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:04.170 align:middle line:90%
with a pretty small crime.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:05.810 align:middle line:84%
And then the 0 here
is where we both

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.430 align:middle line:84%
testify, we both get charged
with a serious crime,

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:12.210 align:middle line:84%
but we get a sentence reduction
because we each cooperated

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:13.690 align:middle line:90%
with the authorities.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:17.010 align:middle line:90%


00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:21.010 align:middle line:84%
Now, I guess the question is,
how would you play in this game?

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:23.010 align:middle line:84%
If you were here, you
were one of the prisoners,

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.550 align:middle line:84%
you're alone in your cell, how
would you think about this?

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:28.310 align:middle line:84%
And how would you expect
the other person to play?

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:34.130 align:middle line:90%


00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.290 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] go F.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.370 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Say again?

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:36.890 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] go F.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:37.932 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You would go F?

00:11:37.932 --> 00:11:39.976 align:middle line:90%
OK, tell me why.

00:11:39.976 --> 00:11:42.390 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Because the other
one doesn't, then you're

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.070 align:middle line:90%
just [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.550 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So you're
saying you want

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.110 align:middle line:84%
to go F because you're really
afraid of-- so let's say

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:50.970 align:middle line:90%
you're player 1.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.830 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to say, if
I go F, you're saying, well,

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.250 align:middle line:90%
I can either get 3 or 0.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.130 align:middle line:84%
But I'm at least not
going to get negative 1.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.163 align:middle line:90%
So that's good.

00:11:59.163 --> 00:12:00.830 align:middle line:84%
So certainly, the
worst possible outcome

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.890 align:middle line:84%
is better if I go F. I
think F is a good strategy.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Any other thoughts about
why I might want to go F?

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:09.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:10.790 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Regardless of what
the other player chooses,

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:11.650 align:middle line:90%
you're better off.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:12.323 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:12:12.323 --> 00:12:13.990 align:middle line:84%
So this is a really
critical observation

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:15.390 align:middle line:90%
to make in this game.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.110 align:middle line:90%
So I'm player 1.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:19.830 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
the other prisoner

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.543 align:middle line:90%
is going to mum or fink.

00:12:21.543 --> 00:12:23.710 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if they're
going to testify against me.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.550 align:middle line:84%
But let me reason
separately by cases, OK?

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:29.070 align:middle line:84%
One possibility is
the other prisoner

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:30.750 align:middle line:90%
is going to stay silent.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.350 align:middle line:84%
If the other prisoner stays
silent, well, what happens?

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.580 align:middle line:84%
Either I get a payoff
of 2, if I stay silent,

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.340 align:middle line:84%
or I get a payoff
of 3, if I testify.

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:41.260 align:middle line:90%
So I'm better off testifying.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:42.820 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's look
at the other case.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:46.040 align:middle line:84%
What if the other prisoner is
going to testify against me?

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.360 align:middle line:90%
That's certainly worse for me.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.660 align:middle line:90%
I'm certainly worse off.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.280 align:middle line:84%
But if the other
prisoner testifies, well,

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:54.180 align:middle line:84%
if I stay silent,
I get negative 1.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.820 align:middle line:84%
And if I fink, I get
0, which is higher?

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.580 align:middle line:84%
So in both cases, I do
strictly better if I fink.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:04.180 align:middle line:84%
So often, we think that
a prediction of this game

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:07.660 align:middle line:84%
might be we might be
pretty confident that,

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:08.640 align:middle line:90%
for each player--

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:12.448 align:middle line:90%


00:13:12.448 --> 00:13:13.240 align:middle line:90%
what are we saying?

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:19.700 align:middle line:84%
Well, F is better
than M no matter

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:20.920 align:middle line:90%
what the other player does.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:34.520 align:middle line:90%


00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.780 align:middle line:84%
And in a sense, games like this
are a bit easier to analyze.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.240 align:middle line:84%
Because, remember, we said
what makes game theory hard

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.920 align:middle line:84%
is what we called strategic
interdependence, where

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:44.920 align:middle line:84%
the best thing for
me to do depends

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.360 align:middle line:90%
on what the other player does.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:49.760 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it is an
interactive problem.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:51.640 align:middle line:84%
But the best thing
for me to do is

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.898 align:middle line:84%
independent of what
my opponent does.

00:13:53.898 --> 00:13:55.440 align:middle line:84%
So there actually
is not this element

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.120 align:middle line:90%
of strategic interdependence.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.480 align:middle line:84%
F is better than
M if my opponent

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:04.240 align:middle line:84%
plays M. And F is still better
than M if my opponent plays F.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.080 align:middle line:84%
So I don't really have to think
too carefully about exactly

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:10.320 align:middle line:84%
how my opponent is going to play
because, however they play, I

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:14.200 align:middle line:90%
know I'm better off if I play F.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:16.100 align:middle line:90%
So why is this called a dilemma?

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:18.640 align:middle line:84%
Or sometimes, people
think of it as a paradox.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:23.880 align:middle line:84%
Well, if both players
play F, then--

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:27.840 align:middle line:84%
if I have chalk, but I'll
just highlight this--

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.172 align:middle line:90%
we both get a payoff of 0.

00:14:30.172 --> 00:14:32.380 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's reasonable
for someone to come along

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.380 align:middle line:84%
and say, wait a
second, these prisoners

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:36.380 align:middle line:90%
must be doing something wrong.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:38.340 align:middle line:84%
They're both getting
a payoff of 0.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:42.300 align:middle line:84%
But if they both did M, they'd
both be strictly better off.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:43.508 align:middle line:90%
So are they being irrational?

00:14:43.508 --> 00:14:44.800 align:middle line:90%
Are they doing something wrong?

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.160 align:middle line:90%
In this game, what do you think?

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:48.382 align:middle line:84%
I mean, why is this a good
prediction of the game?

00:14:48.382 --> 00:14:49.840 align:middle line:84%
We said we're
studying game theory.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.360 align:middle line:84%
We said people
are very rational.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:53.040 align:middle line:84%
They act towards
their objectives.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.060 align:middle line:84%
And if they both
do this, they're

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:55.160 align:middle line:90%
both strictly better off.

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.900 align:middle line:90%
So why don't they do that?

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:58.400 align:middle line:84%
Is something wrong
with game theory?

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.940 align:middle line:90%


00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:01.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:05.020 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If one player doesn't
know what the other player is

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:09.100 align:middle line:84%
going to do, they're
just operating in like

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:14.300 align:middle line:84%
just maximize my utility
independent of what

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:15.840 align:middle line:90%
the other person chooses.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:16.540 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:17.915 align:middle line:84%
And another way
of saying it is I

00:15:17.915 --> 00:15:19.880 align:middle line:84%
can't influence what the
other person chooses.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:22.200 align:middle line:84%
So when I framed it, I
said, oh, we're down here.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.420 align:middle line:90%
Wouldn't we like to be up here?

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:25.920 align:middle line:90%
But how do we get up here?

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:28.602 align:middle line:84%
Well, both players would
have to behave differently.

00:15:28.602 --> 00:15:30.560 align:middle line:84%
And I can't control what
the other person does.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.480 align:middle line:90%
I can only control what I do.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.360 align:middle line:84%
And I think this observation was
surprising to a lot of people

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:36.900 align:middle line:90%
early on.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:39.140 align:middle line:84%
I mean, mathematically,
it's not very deep.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:40.840 align:middle line:84%
But it definitely
surprised people.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:44.400 align:middle line:84%
And I think the key
interpretation of the prisoner's

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:51.720 align:middle line:84%
dilemma, or what we learned
from it, is that, in games,

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:56.280 align:middle line:84%
rational behavior can
lead to inefficiency.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:01.920 align:middle line:84%
Or more precisely,
rational behavior

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.380 align:middle line:90%
can make everyone worse off.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:14.600 align:middle line:90%


00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.143 align:middle line:84%
Worse off than
what, you might say?

00:16:16.143 --> 00:16:18.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, what I mean is
than some other behavior.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:26.920 align:middle line:90%


00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:29.790 align:middle line:84%
And this is a novel
implication or a novel feature

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.670 align:middle line:90%
of interactive decision making.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.830 align:middle line:84%
In a single decision maker
problem, this never happens.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.650 align:middle line:84%
In a single agent problem, I
just do what's best for me.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.630 align:middle line:84%
And that gives me the
best possible outcome.

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:42.910 align:middle line:84%
But with an interactive problem,
even if we're both behaving,

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:45.950 align:middle line:84%
quote unquote, "rationally," the
outcome may be very bad for both

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:48.910 align:middle line:84%
of us and may be worse than a
different outcome that would be

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.410 align:middle line:90%
feasible for both of us.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:56.470 align:middle line:90%


00:16:56.470 --> 00:17:00.710 align:middle line:84%
So I think today we want to
continue what we started here.

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:04.829 align:middle line:84%
And we argued that this
game was relatively

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:08.030 align:middle line:84%
easy to analyze because of
this property, that the best

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:10.727 align:middle line:84%
thing for me is independent
of what the other player does.

00:17:10.727 --> 00:17:13.310 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's a guide for
the way we're going to approach

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:14.730 align:middle line:90%
analysis in this class.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.630 align:middle line:84%
So moving forward,
we're going to start

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:23.950 align:middle line:84%
with minimal assumptions,
minimal assumptions

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:24.890 align:middle line:90%
about behavior.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:31.570 align:middle line:90%


00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:34.090 align:middle line:84%
And because these are
minimal or weak assumptions,

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:37.010 align:middle line:84%
we're going to be quite
confident in the implications

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:38.170 align:middle line:90%
of these assumptions.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.790 align:middle line:84%
So this might make us
confident about predictions.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:48.450 align:middle line:90%


00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.250 align:middle line:84%
But what we'll see is
that a lot of games

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.010 align:middle line:84%
are not like the
prisoner's dilemma.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.250 align:middle line:84%
And we're not able to make very
sharp predictions only based

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:56.610 align:middle line:90%
on minimal assumptions.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.710 align:middle line:84%
So we might be confident
about our predictions.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.168 align:middle line:84%
But these predictions
are generally

00:18:00.168 --> 00:18:01.970 align:middle line:84%
going to be very
weak predictions.

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:04.070 align:middle line:84%
Or they could be very
weak predictions.

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.650 align:middle line:90%


00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And here, notice, I'm using the
interpretation of prediction

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:13.490 align:middle line:84%
rather than
recommendation for now.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:15.370 align:middle line:84%
In fact, sometimes,
if we're only

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.170 align:middle line:84%
willing to make minimal
assumptions about behavior,

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:20.810 align:middle line:84%
our only prediction might
be anything could happen.

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:22.350 align:middle line:84%
That's not a very
useful prediction.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:24.950 align:middle line:84%
We might not be able to
narrow things down very much.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.790 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to do, over
the course of the class,

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:30.430 align:middle line:84%
is gradually strengthen
the assumptions

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.110 align:middle line:90%
that we make about behavior.

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.550 align:middle line:84%
We might gradually
lose some confidence

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.330 align:middle line:84%
in the accuracy of
these predictions.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:38.705 align:middle line:84%
But at the same time, we'll
be able to sharpen and refine

00:18:38.705 --> 00:18:39.913 align:middle line:90%
the predictions that we make.

00:18:39.913 --> 00:18:41.710 align:middle line:84%
And that's the direction
we're going to go.

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.377 align:middle line:84%
So today, we're going
to start with what

00:18:43.377 --> 00:18:45.670 align:middle line:84%
I think of as the most
basic or most minimal

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.190 align:middle line:90%
assumption about behavior.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:50.130 align:middle line:84%
And later on, we'll move to
more complicated assumptions.

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:54.990 align:middle line:90%


00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:57.750 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing I need to do
is just introduce some notation

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.910 align:middle line:90%
for strategic form games.

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.990 align:middle line:84%
And then I can formally
define and capture

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:05.990 align:middle line:90%
what we described in this game.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:09.533 align:middle line:84%
So strategic form games
often have many players.

00:19:09.533 --> 00:19:10.950 align:middle line:84%
But we're often
interested in what

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:13.770 align:middle line:84%
happens when a single player
modifies their behavior,

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:15.070 align:middle line:90%
modifies their strategy.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.870 align:middle line:84%
So we'd like to have some
notation to capture that.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:26.500 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to write
is a strategy profile will often

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:28.180 align:middle line:90%
decompose it in this way.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:32.040 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say we can think of the
profile as player i's strategy.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.140 align:middle line:90%


00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:38.040 align:middle line:84%
And S negative i's specifies
the strategies of everyone else.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:44.780 align:middle line:90%


00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:46.120 align:middle line:90%
And this notation is important.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:48.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's just go through an
example with three players.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:54.020 align:middle line:84%
If S equals S1, S2, S3,
this strategy profile

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.802 align:middle line:84%
specifies a strategy for
each of the three players.

00:19:56.802 --> 00:19:58.260 align:middle line:84%
But there's three
different ways we

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.220 align:middle line:84%
can express this
depending on which player

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:01.700 align:middle line:90%
we want to focus on.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:06.300 align:middle line:84%
So we could write this
as S1, S negative 1,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.400 align:middle line:84%
meaning we're going to put
player 1's strategy by itself.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:12.540 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to group
the other two players' strategy

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.300 align:middle line:84%
under this notation
S negative 1.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.080 align:middle line:84%
Here, negative 1 is not
really the number negative 1.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:19.860 align:middle line:90%
What we mean is not player 1.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.578 align:middle line:84%
So think of the minus here
as not rather than minus.

00:20:24.578 --> 00:20:26.620 align:middle line:84%
But then there's another
way we could write this.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:32.600 align:middle line:84%
We could write it as S2
S negative 2 or as S3 S

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:34.800 align:middle line:90%
negative 3.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.467 align:middle line:84%
And you might object that, well,
S3 is supposed to come last.

00:20:37.467 --> 00:20:39.800 align:middle line:84%
We're not really going to be
consistent about the order.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:41.920 align:middle line:84%
As long as we understand
what this means,

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:45.200 align:middle line:84%
this is a strategy
profile where player three

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:48.200 align:middle line:84%
chooses S3 and players
1 and 2 collectively

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.308 align:middle line:90%
choose S negative 3.

00:20:49.308 --> 00:20:50.100 align:middle line:90%
It's just notation.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:52.500 align:middle line:84%
But it's going to help us
write things down more cleanly.

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:53.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.760 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So I need to add
more of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.720 align:middle line:90%
first, [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:00.040 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Sorry,
I don't mean that.

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:01.857 align:middle line:84%
I'm not making any--
it's just notation,

00:21:01.857 --> 00:21:02.940 align:middle line:90%
no behavioral assumptions.

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:04.980 align:middle line:84%
So literally, when I
write S negative 3,

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:09.000 align:middle line:90%
what I mean is S1, S2.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.420 align:middle line:90%
It's just notation.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.160 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not claiming that they're
going to decide together.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:15.660 align:middle line:84%
Or I'm not doing
anything like that.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.300 align:middle line:84%
It's just to save
space on the board.

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:20.640 align:middle line:84%
Instead of writing S1, S2, I'm
going to write S negative 3.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.320 align:middle line:84%
But they're still choosing
independently of each other.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:24.640 align:middle line:90%
That's a good clarification.

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:27.020 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.980 align:middle line:84%
And then we need some notation
for where these things live.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:31.780 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to use notation.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:33.660 align:middle line:90%
We've already introduced Si.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:35.820 align:middle line:84%
But now, we're going
to use S negative i

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.900 align:middle line:84%
to be the set of-- you might
call them partial strategy

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:39.800 align:middle line:90%
profiles.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:41.700 align:middle line:84%
These are the set of
profiles specifying

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:44.780 align:middle line:84%
a strategy for every
player except player i.

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:47.820 align:middle line:90%
So S little i lives in SI.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:51.260 align:middle line:84%
And S negative i, or
not i, lives in S0

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:53.080 align:middle line:84%
So that notation
will be useful below.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:58.284 align:middle line:90%


00:21:58.284 --> 00:22:00.780 align:middle line:84%
So now, we'd like to
formalize what happened

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:02.140 align:middle line:90%
in the prisoner's dilemma.

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:10.700 align:middle line:84%
And the formal term for this
is dominance, a natural term

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:13.020 align:middle line:84%
because F is better
than M no matter

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.540 align:middle line:90%
what the other player does.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.988 align:middle line:84%
We would say that strategy
F dominates strategy M.

00:22:17.988 --> 00:22:20.280 align:middle line:84%
But we have to be a little
more careful because there's

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.620 align:middle line:90%
strict and weak dominance.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:25.400 align:middle line:84%
So now, I'm going to
define these two terms.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:31.440 align:middle line:90%
So for player i--

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:33.080 align:middle line:90%
so let's consider player i.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:36.820 align:middle line:84%
And let's consider
two strategies--

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:39.960 align:middle line:90%


00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.860 align:middle line:90%
Si and Si prime.

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.440 align:middle line:90%


00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.280 align:middle line:84%
And I want to say, so
I'm focusing on player i.

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:50.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm focusing on two strategies--
they could be the same,

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:52.960 align:middle line:90%
but two strategies of player i.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:55.440 align:middle line:90%
This is a prime here.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:59.640 align:middle line:84%
And I want to formally define
what it means for strategy Si

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:03.440 align:middle line:84%
to strictly dominate
strategy S negative i.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:07.580 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to say Si
strictly dominates--

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:10.460 align:middle line:90%


00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:11.960 align:middle line:84%
sorry, I may have
said S negative i.

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:13.000 align:middle line:90%
I mean Si prime.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.550 align:middle line:90%


00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:18.230 align:middle line:84%
Can anyone guess in
words, strict dominates,

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:21.150 align:middle line:84%
what that might mean, just if we
think through from the example,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.110 align:middle line:84%
what it might mean for player
i if strategy Si strictly

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:27.460 align:middle line:90%
dominates Si prime?

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:28.210 align:middle line:90%
Maybe at the back?

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:28.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:30.550 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: They have
a better payoff

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.910 align:middle line:90%
regardless of S negative i.

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:33.910 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:36.590 align:middle line:90%
And by better, strictly better.

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:40.430 align:middle line:90%
So Si dominates Si prime means--

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:44.330 align:middle line:84%
sometimes I'll use this arrow
just to mean a definition.

00:23:44.330 --> 00:23:47.790 align:middle line:84%
So I'm saying this is exactly
what this phrase means.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:51.510 align:middle line:84%
It means that-- well, let
me first just partially

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:52.290 align:middle line:90%
write it out.

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:57.790 align:middle line:90%


00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:00.152 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to fill
it in in a second.

00:24:00.152 --> 00:24:02.110 align:middle line:84%
But the first thing is
I'm looking-- let's just

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:03.630 align:middle line:90%
focus on what's here already.

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:05.750 align:middle line:90%
I'm focusing on player i.

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:08.430 align:middle line:84%
And player i is making a
comparison between things

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:10.430 align:middle line:90%
that are in player i's control.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:13.330 align:middle line:84%
Player i is comparing
what happens

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:17.810 align:middle line:84%
if she plays Si to what
happens if she plays Si prime.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:20.930 align:middle line:84%
But what we want to argue is
that this inequality holds

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:23.290 align:middle line:90%
whatever the opponents play.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to fill
in S negative i here

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:27.610 align:middle line:90%
and S negative i here.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:33.730 align:middle line:84%
And we want this to hold for all
S negative i in S negative I.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.250 align:middle line:90%
So notice a key point here.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.970 align:middle line:84%
S negative i can take
any possible value.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.570 align:middle line:84%
But in the inequality,
the same S negative i

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:43.130 align:middle line:90%
appears on both sides.

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:45.410 align:middle line:84%
So fixing the way that
my opponent is playing,

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:50.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm strictly preferred to
play Si to playing Si prime.

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.450 align:middle line:90%
Why do we write it this way?

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:53.890 align:middle line:84%
Because S negative i's
outside my control.

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:55.790 align:middle line:84%
I can't control what
my opponents are doing.

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:57.130 align:middle line:90%
But they're doing something.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:01.378 align:middle line:84%
So however they're behaving,
this inequality should hold.

00:25:01.378 --> 00:25:03.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, we want to give a slightly
weaker version of this.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:04.850 align:middle line:90%
This is very demanding.

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:06.090 align:middle line:90%
And it may not hold.

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:09.370 align:middle line:84%
So let's now say
weakly dominates.

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:14.790 align:middle line:84%
I won't fill out everything
else, but just copy these words.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:18.430 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going to weaken
this to weak inequality.

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:30.990 align:middle line:90%


00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:34.870 align:middle line:84%
But this is a little too weak
because this allows these two

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:38.370 align:middle line:84%
strategies to give me the same
payoff whatever my opponents do.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And we want there to be some
reason that Si is better than Si

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:41.670 align:middle line:90%
prime.

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.190 align:middle line:84%
So we demand this
weak inequality

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:52.430 align:middle line:84%
for every S negative i,
but strict inequality

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:53.890 align:middle line:90%
for some S negative i.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:57.310 align:middle line:90%


00:25:57.310 --> 00:26:00.350 align:middle line:84%
So however my
opponents play, Si does

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:02.190 align:middle line:90%
weakly better than Si prime.

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:04.430 align:middle line:84%
But there's some, at
least one particular way

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:08.940 align:middle line:84%
my opponents could play such
that Si is just strictly better

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:11.620 align:middle line:90%
than Si prime.

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:13.700 align:middle line:84%
And in particular,
the strict part of it

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:18.300 align:middle line:84%
means that no strategy can
weakly dominate itself,

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.740 align:middle line:84%
because if we put the same
strategy here for Si and Si

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:24.400 align:middle line:84%
prime, then we would never
get a strict inequality.

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:27.780 align:middle line:90%


00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:31.760 align:middle line:84%
So far, these definitions have
been pairwise comparisons.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:33.900 align:middle line:84%
We've taken one strategy
and we've compared it

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:35.300 align:middle line:90%
to another strategy.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:37.960 align:middle line:84%
But in reality, the player just
needs to choose one strategy.

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:39.335 align:middle line:84%
So what we'd like
to know is when

00:26:39.335 --> 00:26:42.060 align:middle line:84%
is one strategy better than
everything else or dominates

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:43.140 align:middle line:90%
everything else.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:47.400 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say a strategy
Si is weakly dominant--

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:52.180 align:middle line:90%


00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:53.660 align:middle line:90%
we could do weakly or strictly.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:27:00.525 align:middle line:90%


00:27:00.525 --> 00:27:04.940 align:middle line:84%
Si is weakly dominant if Si
weakly or strictly dominates

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.360 align:middle line:84%
every other strategy
that player i has.

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:10.000 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll just do
weakly to avoid confusion.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:22.560 align:middle line:84%
We say Si is weakly dominant
if Si weakly dominates

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:27.162 align:middle line:90%
every strategy Si prime.

00:27:27.162 --> 00:27:28.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, I need to be
a little careful.

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:29.960 align:middle line:90%
There's something wrong here.

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.000 align:middle line:84%
I can't say every
strategy, because Si

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:33.320 align:middle line:90%
can't dominate itself.

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:41.760 align:middle line:84%
So every strategy Si prime
that's not equal to C.

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:44.840 align:middle line:84%
So we think that, in this
case, if player i has

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:46.840 align:middle line:84%
a weakly dominant
strategy, that's

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:50.120 align:middle line:84%
a pretty safe prediction
about how that player will

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:52.940 align:middle line:84%
play in the game, about which
strategy that player will use.

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:57.560 align:middle line:90%


00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.860 align:middle line:84%
And we have one
final definition.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:02.800 align:middle line:90%


00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:05.260 align:middle line:84%
And that is, well, let's
think about the simplest

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:06.460 align:middle line:90%
possible case.

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:10.820 align:middle line:84%
That's where every player
has some dominant strategy,

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:12.843 align:middle line:84%
has some weakly
dominant strategy.

00:28:12.843 --> 00:28:14.260 align:middle line:84%
If that's the case,
then we can be

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:16.860 align:middle line:84%
pretty confident our
prediction in that game

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:18.740 align:middle line:84%
is that each player
is going to play

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:22.140 align:middle line:84%
their corresponding
weakly dominant strategy.

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So we can give a
definition here,

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.980 align:middle line:84%
our first notion
of an equilibrium.

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:34.380 align:middle line:84%
We say a profile S1
star up to Sn star

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:36.640 align:middle line:84%
is a dominant
strategy equilibrium.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:45.735 align:middle line:90%


00:28:45.735 --> 00:28:47.860 align:middle line:84%
And here, I'm just going
to write dominant strategy

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:48.840 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:52.140 align:middle line:84%
But more precisely, we mean
weakly dominant strategy

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:53.020 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:54.480 align:middle line:90%
But we'll just say dominant.

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:56.080 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, we'll
call this DSE--

00:28:56.080 --> 00:29:00.740 align:middle line:90%


00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:02.120 align:middle line:90%
if what?

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.780 align:middle line:90%
Well, this is a profile.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:05.855 align:middle line:84%
So we basically
made a prediction

00:29:05.855 --> 00:29:07.980 align:middle line:84%
about how each player in
the game is going to play.

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:10.640 align:middle line:84%
We've made a prediction
about each player's strategy.

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.320 align:middle line:84%
And we say that this is a
dominant strategy equilibrium

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:16.240 align:middle line:84%
if each player is
playing a strategy that's

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.120 align:middle line:84%
a weakly dominant
strategy for that player.

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:29.440 align:middle line:84%
So if, for each
player i, strategy--

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:32.040 align:middle line:84%
well, what is the
strategy used by player i?

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:33.440 align:middle line:90%
It's Si star.

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:37.700 align:middle line:84%
So strategy Si star
is weakly dominant.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:45.463 align:middle line:90%


00:29:45.463 --> 00:29:46.880 align:middle line:84%
But notice an
important thing here

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:49.920 align:middle line:84%
that, when we make a
prediction about a game,

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.977 align:middle line:84%
it's not enough just to
predict one player's strategy.

00:29:52.977 --> 00:29:54.560 align:middle line:84%
We have to make a
prediction about how

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:56.280 align:middle line:90%
every player is going to play.

00:29:56.280 --> 00:29:58.440 align:middle line:84%
So our prediction
here is a profile

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:01.070 align:middle line:84%
of strategies specifying
a strategy for each

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:02.230 align:middle line:90%
of the n players.

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:07.090 align:middle line:84%
And it's a dominant strategy
equilibrium if, for each player,

00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:09.990 align:middle line:84%
their strategy is weakly
dominant for them,

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:13.830 align:middle line:84%
meaning that strategy, Si star--
if we go back to our definition,

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:18.510 align:middle line:84%
Si star weakly dominates every
other strategy by player i.

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:19.410 align:middle line:90%
Any question on this?

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.110 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:26.190 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is it possible to
have multiple weakly dominant

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:28.450 align:middle line:84%
strategies, like one better
than a different one?

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:29.170 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: That's
a great question.

00:30:29.170 --> 00:30:30.390 align:middle line:90%
So let's put this here.

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:31.590 align:middle line:84%
Let's write this on
the board because I

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:32.548 align:middle line:90%
was going to get there.

00:30:32.548 --> 00:30:33.870 align:middle line:90%
That's a great question.

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:37.470 align:middle line:84%
Is it possible for there to
be multiple weakly dominant

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:38.010 align:middle line:90%
strategies?

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:40.890 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll say WD strategies.

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:43.550 align:middle line:90%


00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.350 align:middle line:90%
It's a great question.

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:47.290 align:middle line:90%
Any ideas?

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:49.950 align:middle line:90%
I'll put it to the class.

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:51.295 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:30:51.295 --> 00:30:52.630 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:52.630 --> 00:30:54.040 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Say it again?

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:54.873 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:54.873 --> 00:30:56.995 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:30:56.995 --> 00:30:58.370 align:middle line:84%
So what you have
in mind is, what

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.953 align:middle line:84%
if there's two strategies that
give me exactly the same payoff

00:31:00.953 --> 00:31:02.410 align:middle line:90%
however my opponent plays?

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:04.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have to
be really careful.

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:07.490 align:middle line:84%
The key thing, though, is
this part of the definition.

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:09.713 align:middle line:84%
So if we didn't have this
part of the definition,

00:31:09.713 --> 00:31:12.130 align:middle line:84%
we could have two weak-- we
could have two strategies that

00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:15.210 align:middle line:84%
were both weakly
dominant because they

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:18.090 align:middle line:84%
give the same payoff
however the opponents play.

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:20.050 align:middle line:84%
But two strategies that
give the same payoff

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:23.730 align:middle line:84%
are not going to satisfy
strict inequality for any way

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:24.970 align:middle line:90%
my opponents are playing.

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:28.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's not possible for two
strategies-- for one strategy

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.770 align:middle line:84%
to weakly dominate another
and for that strategy

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:32.350 align:middle line:90%
to weakly dominate the first.

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.090 align:middle line:84%
And for that reason,
it's impossible

00:31:34.090 --> 00:31:36.590 align:middle line:84%
for there to be multiple
weakly dominant strategies.

00:31:36.590 --> 00:31:39.170 align:middle line:90%
So the answer is no.

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:40.670 align:middle line:84%
Let's go through
the argument again.

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:44.030 align:middle line:84%
Suppose I had two strategies
that were both weakly dominant.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:46.770 align:middle line:90%
Let's call them SA and SB.

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, that means SA
must weakly dominate SB.

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:53.890 align:middle line:90%
And SB must weakly dominate SA.

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:56.750 align:middle line:84%
But that means SB must do weakly
weekly better than SA no matter

00:31:56.750 --> 00:32:00.230 align:middle line:84%
how my opponents do, but
sometimes strictly better.

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:02.750 align:middle line:84%
But if SB sometimes
does strictly better,

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:06.790 align:middle line:84%
then it can't be the case
that SA weakly dominates SB.

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:09.227 align:middle line:84%
Maybe a bit fast, it might
be a good little exercise

00:32:09.227 --> 00:32:09.810 align:middle line:90%
to write down.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:10.977 align:middle line:90%
But that's a great question.

00:32:10.977 --> 00:32:12.290 align:middle line:90%
And it's good to keep in mind.

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:14.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's one of
our motivations

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:17.470 align:middle line:84%
for demanding strict inequality
for some S negative i,

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:20.070 align:middle line:84%
to rule out this case of
multiple weakly dominant

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:21.510 align:middle line:90%
strategies.

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:22.010 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:22.890 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:23.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:23.670 --> 00:32:26.790 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just to clarify, for
the [INAUDIBLE] for strictly

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:31.230 align:middle line:84%
dominates, is it just
that the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:35.270 align:middle line:90%


00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:35.990 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:39.190 align:middle line:90%
So what I mean is let's list--

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:41.310 align:middle line:84%
for each S negative i,
we have an inequality

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:42.397 align:middle line:90%
that looks like this.

00:32:42.397 --> 00:32:43.730 align:middle line:90%
So let's say we have 10 of them.

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:45.980 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just going to go
through each of them and say,

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:47.790 align:middle line:84%
does equality hold
in every single one

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:49.030 align:middle line:90%
of those inequalities?

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:53.350 align:middle line:84%
And to get weak dominance,
equality cannot hold in every

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:54.070 align:middle line:90%
single one.

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:55.570 align:middle line:84%
So another way of
saying it is there

00:32:55.570 --> 00:32:57.510 align:middle line:84%
must be one inequality
where it's strict.

00:32:57.510 --> 00:32:59.710 align:middle line:84%
But it could be
equality in all but one.

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:00.450 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:06.810 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so when I say strict, I'm
referring to that being strict.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:07.310 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:08.650 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:13.720 align:middle line:90%


00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:16.050 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's look at a
slightly more complicated game

00:33:16.050 --> 00:33:19.510 align:middle line:84%
than the prisoner's dilemma and
see how we would approach this.

00:33:19.510 --> 00:33:27.818 align:middle line:90%


00:33:27.818 --> 00:33:31.370 align:middle line:84%
Let me make sure I get
[INAUDIBLE] two on top.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:32.910 align:middle line:90%
So we have a two player game.

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:41.890 align:middle line:90%


00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:44.950 align:middle line:84%
So remember, we, always
put player 1 here.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:47.170 align:middle line:84%
Player 1 is always
choosing rows.

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:49.530 align:middle line:84%
Player 2 is always
choosing columns.

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.450 align:middle line:84%
And when we fill in
the payoffs, we always

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:54.040 align:middle line:90%
put player 1's payoffs first.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.330 align:middle line:84%
So here, player 1
has three strategies.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:33:58.080 align:middle line:84%
We call them top,
middle, and bottom, just

00:33:58.080 --> 00:34:00.080 align:middle line:90%
to make it easy to remember.

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.080 align:middle line:84%
And player 2 also
has two strategies,

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:03.760 align:middle line:90%
which we call left and right.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.200 align:middle line:84%
So for what I'm
going to do here,

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:07.500 align:middle line:84%
I really only care about
player 1's payoffs.

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:10.139 align:middle line:90%
So to avoid just distractions.

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:13.300 align:middle line:84%
I'm only going to fill
in player 1's payoffs.

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:16.802 align:middle line:90%


00:34:16.802 --> 00:34:18.219 align:middle line:84%
So there should
be something here.

00:34:18.219 --> 00:34:20.318 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put this
to indicate, if I

00:34:20.318 --> 00:34:21.860 align:middle line:84%
wanted to finish
filling in the game,

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:23.402 align:middle line:84%
I'd have to put in
all those numbers.

00:34:23.402 --> 00:34:24.880 align:middle line:84%
But for what I'm
doing now, I only

00:34:24.880 --> 00:34:26.500 align:middle line:90%
care about these first numbers.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:30.480 align:middle line:90%


00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:35.753 align:middle line:84%
So let's ask, in this game, does
any strategy dominate any other?

00:34:35.753 --> 00:34:37.920 align:middle line:84%
Does it strictly dominate,
weakly dominate any other

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:38.780 align:middle line:90%
for player 1?

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:43.679 align:middle line:90%


00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:44.500 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:50.487 align:middle line:90%


00:34:50.487 --> 00:34:51.820 align:middle line:90%
How do you feel about this game?

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:53.520 align:middle line:84%
Would you be happy to play
any of these strategies?

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.800 align:middle line:84%
Or do you think some of
them seem worse than others?

00:34:55.800 --> 00:35:03.900 align:middle line:90%


00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:05.560 align:middle line:90%
OK, maybe we'll go on.

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.320 align:middle line:84%
But the answer is nothing
dominates anything else.

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:09.380 align:middle line:90%
Let's go through it.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:11.420 align:middle line:90%
Does T dominate M?

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:13.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, you might think so
because 2 is greater than 0.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:16.180 align:middle line:84%
But over here, we
have negative 1 and 0.

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:21.020 align:middle line:84%
So M does better than T
if my opponent plays R.

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:25.020 align:middle line:84%
So it can't be the case that
T dominates M. And similarly,

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:26.860 align:middle line:90%
does M dominate B?

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, even though M does better
than B when my opponent plays L,

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:34.900 align:middle line:84%
M does worse than B when
my opponent plays R.

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:36.900 align:middle line:84%
And you can go through
each of these comparisons

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:41.020 align:middle line:84%
and see that no strategy weakly
dominates any other strategy.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:43.400 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have a
bit of a conundrum.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:44.780 align:middle line:84%
How should you
play in this game?

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:45.530 align:middle line:90%
It's not so clear.

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:46.760 align:middle line:90%
Before, it was pretty easy.

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:49.052 align:middle line:84%
In the prisoner's dilemma,
we said, if you're rational,

00:35:49.052 --> 00:35:51.460 align:middle line:84%
play F. You don't have to
worry about your opponent.

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:54.120 align:middle line:84%
However they play, you're
strictly better off playing F.

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.655 align:middle line:90%
But here, it's not so clear.

00:35:56.655 --> 00:35:58.280 align:middle line:84%
So how would you
approach this problem,

00:35:58.280 --> 00:35:59.570 align:middle line:90%
if you were player 1 here?

00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:00.820 align:middle line:90%
What would be relevant to you?

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:02.278 align:middle line:84%
What would you need
to think about?

00:36:02.278 --> 00:36:05.120 align:middle line:90%


00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:05.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.160 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You want to know what
the chances are the other player

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:10.080 align:middle line:90%
plays [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:10.480 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:12.105 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to
have to form beliefs

00:36:12.105 --> 00:36:13.500 align:middle line:84%
about how the
other player plays,

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.200 align:middle line:84%
just like on the first class
when we said whether you walk

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:19.480 align:middle line:84%
home or take the T depends on,
after checking your phone, what

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:21.990 align:middle line:84%
you think the probability
is that it's going to rain.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:24.240 align:middle line:84%
In the same way, we're going
to form beliefs or assign

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:28.360 align:middle line:84%
probabilities to the strategies
that my opponent is playing.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:35.240 align:middle line:84%
So in this context, maybe we'll
assign a probability of p to L

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:40.560 align:middle line:84%
and 1 minus p to R. So now,
let's compute my payoffs

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:44.040 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to write u of L, p.

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:45.630 align:middle line:90%
So a few things here--

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:48.270 align:middle line:84%
because my opponent
only has two strategies,

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:51.390 align:middle line:84%
if I want to specify my
belief about their strategy,

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:53.483 align:middle line:84%
it's enough to specify
a single number p.

00:36:53.483 --> 00:36:54.650 align:middle line:90%
And I want to be clear here.

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:55.770 align:middle line:90%
This is a number p.

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:56.910 align:middle line:90%
It's not a vector.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:58.030 align:middle line:90%
It's just a number.

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:00.950 align:middle line:84%
Once I know how much
probability I assigned to L,

00:37:00.950 --> 00:37:04.350 align:middle line:84%
then the amount of probability
I assign to R is always just 1

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:05.770 align:middle line:90%
minus that probability.

00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:08.030 align:middle line:90%
So this is enough information.

00:37:08.030 --> 00:37:10.290 align:middle line:84%
And then here,
remember, technically--

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:11.450 align:middle line:90%
I'll put 1 here--

00:37:11.450 --> 00:37:15.310 align:middle line:84%
we only define the utility
u1 of a strategy profile.

00:37:15.310 --> 00:37:17.790 align:middle line:84%
So technically, we haven't
defined what this means yet.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:19.790 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to
extend our notation

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:23.070 align:middle line:84%
to define what my
expected payoff is

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.550 align:middle line:84%
if I play L and I believe
that my opponent is going

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:30.230 align:middle line:84%
to play L with probability p and
R with probability 1 minus p.

00:37:30.230 --> 00:37:30.870 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:33.063 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You're playing T
or M or R [? instead. ?]

00:37:33.063 --> 00:37:34.230 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Ah, yes, T, sorry.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:35.190 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:36.950 align:middle line:90%
T, yes.

00:37:36.950 --> 00:37:38.590 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:39.550 align:middle line:90%
So here we are.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:42.710 align:middle line:84%
So what is it/ Well,
with probability p,

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:47.910 align:middle line:84%
my opponent plays L. And
therefore, my payoff is u1 of T,

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:55.090 align:middle line:84%
L. But with probability 1
minus p, my opponent plays R.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:58.650 align:middle line:84%
I'm still playing T. Thanks
for the clarification.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:04.650 align:middle line:90%
So we have T, R.

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:06.290 align:middle line:84%
So notice, we're
treating my beliefs

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:08.970 align:middle line:84%
about my opponent's strategy
just like we treated lotteries

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:09.770 align:middle line:90%
before.

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:12.090 align:middle line:84%
When I have beliefs
about something,

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:14.850 align:middle line:84%
I think about my
choices as corresponding

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.450 align:middle line:90%
to lotteries over outcomes.

00:38:17.450 --> 00:38:20.730 align:middle line:84%
And then I evaluate expected
utility over those outcomes.

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:22.630 align:middle line:84%
So here, we can just
plug in the numbers.

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:24.730 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get this
is going to be a 2.

00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:28.170 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be a negative 1.

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.270 align:middle line:90%
So what do we get?

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:37.850 align:middle line:84%
We get 2p minus 1 plus p,
if we do the math right,

00:38:37.850 --> 00:38:40.990 align:middle line:90%
which equals 3p minus 1.

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:43.550 align:middle line:90%


00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:46.750 align:middle line:84%
And it's often good to
check if this makes sense.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:48.510 align:middle line:90%
The higher is p--

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:49.510 align:middle line:90%
yes?

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:51.430 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So I just
[INAUDIBLE] question--

00:38:51.430 --> 00:38:54.110 align:middle line:84%
the utility, in this
case, is specifically only

00:38:54.110 --> 00:38:56.330 align:middle line:84%
for the lottery, which is
represented by the value p.

00:38:56.330 --> 00:38:58.430 align:middle line:84%
But we also have to
include strategy, right?

00:38:58.430 --> 00:38:59.310 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:01.018 align:middle line:84%
So if we want to be
a little more formal,

00:39:01.018 --> 00:39:02.950 align:middle line:84%
what is this/ This
is the lottery.

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:04.890 align:middle line:90%
Well, this is a two player game.

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:08.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is a lottery over the
strategies of my opponents.

00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:14.830 align:middle line:84%
So this lives in delta
of, in this case, S2,

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:19.070 align:middle line:84%
but, more generally, S negative
1, because what I need to do

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:21.090 align:middle line:84%
is I need to say,
more generally,

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:23.790 align:middle line:84%
of all the possible
profiles of strategies

00:39:23.790 --> 00:39:25.670 align:middle line:84%
that my opponents
could play, I need

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:29.070 align:middle line:84%
to form a belief that specifies
the probability on each of them.

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:32.010 align:middle line:84%
In this case, I can represent
that belief by a single number.

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:34.210 align:middle line:84%
But more generally, it's going
to be a more complicated object.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:36.293 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So that means
utilities usually calculate

00:39:36.293 --> 00:39:40.250 align:middle line:84%
the cross-product of a
strategy [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:41.350 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes, yes.

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:43.650 align:middle line:84%
And I'll be more formal
in a second, yeah.

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:44.552 align:middle line:90%
Other question, yeah?

00:39:44.552 --> 00:39:46.010 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So is the
utility function

00:39:46.010 --> 00:39:48.050 align:middle line:90%
on the left side [INAUDIBLE]?

00:39:48.050 --> 00:39:51.375 align:middle line:90%


00:39:51.375 --> 00:39:52.750 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Good
point, good point.

00:39:52.750 --> 00:39:56.123 align:middle line:84%
People are being very,
very precise here.

00:39:56.123 --> 00:39:57.790 align:middle line:84%
You might say it's
an abuse of notation.

00:39:57.790 --> 00:39:59.370 align:middle line:84%
Or you might say we're
extending the notation

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:00.328 align:middle line:90%
on the right-hand side.

00:40:00.328 --> 00:40:02.410 align:middle line:84%
So originally, we
defined utilities only

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.290 align:middle line:90%
for strategy profiles.

00:40:04.290 --> 00:40:06.090 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going
to define-- formally,

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:08.382 align:middle line:84%
what we're doing is we're
going to extend this function

00:40:08.382 --> 00:40:09.510 align:middle line:90%
to a larger domain.

00:40:09.510 --> 00:40:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to imagine
that it has the same meaning

00:40:11.810 --> 00:40:12.910 align:middle line:90%
on the original domain.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:15.370 align:middle line:84%
But now, we're going to
define it more broadly.

00:40:15.370 --> 00:40:18.490 align:middle line:84%
So maybe if I was
being very pedantic,

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:20.390 align:middle line:84%
I would use different
notation here.

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:22.738 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to be
a little less pedantic.

00:40:22.738 --> 00:40:24.530 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
think of these utilities

00:40:24.530 --> 00:40:26.540 align:middle line:84%
as being defined on a
bit more general space.

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:28.290 align:middle line:84%
But you're right that,
conceptually, there

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:29.510 align:middle line:90%
is a distinction here.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:31.450 align:middle line:84%
Though, one way to
think about it is,

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:33.290 align:middle line:90%
how do I interpret u1 of T, L?

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:35.730 align:middle line:84%
One interpretation is this
is what happens when I play T

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:39.320 align:middle line:84%
and I hold the belief that my
opponent is certain to play L.

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:42.378 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of this as a
special case where p equals 1.

00:40:42.378 --> 00:40:43.920 align:middle line:84%
That would be one
way you can see how

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:45.180 align:middle line:90%
these functions fit together.

00:40:45.180 --> 00:40:45.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:46.280 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:46.680 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:49.263 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Can you just use big U
on the left-hand side and then

00:40:49.263 --> 00:40:50.920 align:middle line:90%
little u on the right-hand side?

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:52.460 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: I could do that.

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:53.540 align:middle line:90%
I'm not doing that here.

00:40:53.540 --> 00:40:54.140 align:middle line:90%
I could do it.

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:57.660 align:middle line:84%
But eventually, we just find
it easier to always do this.

00:40:57.660 --> 00:41:00.800 align:middle line:84%
So from now on, we're
going to use little u.

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:03.840 align:middle line:84%
And we can put in a belief
or a strategy over here.

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:05.880 align:middle line:84%
And if we put in a
strategy, the interpretation

00:41:05.880 --> 00:41:08.040 align:middle line:84%
is that's a belief that
puts all the probability

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:09.560 align:middle line:90%
on that particular strategy.

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:10.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:12.140 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions here?

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:12.640 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:14.280 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:15.748 align:middle line:90%
And we could similarly compute--

00:41:15.748 --> 00:41:16.540 align:middle line:90%
I won't do it here.

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:24.080 align:middle line:84%
But we could compute u1
of M, p and u1 of B, p.

00:41:24.080 --> 00:41:25.762 align:middle line:84%
Then I guess the
question suggested

00:41:25.762 --> 00:41:27.720 align:middle line:84%
we need to be a little
more rigorous about what

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:29.260 align:middle line:84%
these beliefs are
and what we mean.

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:31.360 align:middle line:84%
So let me bring
this down and erase.

00:41:31.360 --> 00:41:43.060 align:middle line:90%


00:41:43.060 --> 00:41:45.720 align:middle line:84%
So let's be a little more
precise about what a belief is.

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:48.660 align:middle line:90%


00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:53.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, a belief for player i is
just a probability distribution

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:56.820 align:middle line:84%
over the strategies that the
other players are playing.

00:41:56.820 --> 00:42:04.500 align:middle line:84%
So I'll say, for player
i, it's a function.

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:06.403 align:middle line:90%
Maybe we'll say beta negative i.

00:42:06.403 --> 00:42:08.820 align:middle line:84%
We're going to use the negative
i because even though it's

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:10.278 align:middle line:84%
the belief that's
held by player i,

00:42:10.278 --> 00:42:13.160 align:middle line:84%
it's a belief about
everyone else's strategies.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:15.220 align:middle line:84%
And that's why we use
the negative i here.

00:42:15.220 --> 00:42:15.880 align:middle line:90%
And what is it?

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:19.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's a function
from S negative i

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:23.180 align:middle line:84%
to 0, 1, if you want to be
really formal, where it says,

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:26.060 align:middle line:84%
for each profile of
strategies, or partial profile

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:28.700 align:middle line:84%
of strategies, that
my opponents play,

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:30.500 align:middle line:84%
what is the probability
that I assign

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:33.280 align:middle line:84%
to that particular
profile of strategies?

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:36.080 align:middle line:84%
How likely is it that they
played that particular profile?

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:38.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we need--
we have to be careful.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:41.540 align:middle line:84%
For this to make sense, when we
sum up all these probabilities,

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:43.520 align:middle line:90%
we have to get 1.

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:49.640 align:middle line:84%
So it's a function like
this that's satisfying what?

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:52.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, the total
probability is 1.

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:53.880 align:middle line:90%
So let me take a summation.

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:55.360 align:middle line:90%
What am I summing over?

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:57.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm summing over all
the partial strategy

00:42:57.640 --> 00:42:59.480 align:middle line:90%
profiles of my opponent.

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:04.200 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to sum over s
negative i in big S negative i.

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:06.640 align:middle line:84%
If you're not used to seeing
summations that aren't just

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:08.720 align:middle line:84%
numbers 1 to n, think
of this as just I'm

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:15.560 align:middle line:84%
writing out addition with one
term for each strategy profile

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:18.480 align:middle line:90%
S negative i.

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:22.980 align:middle line:84%
And we have beta negative
i of S negative i equals 1.

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:26.680 align:middle line:90%


00:43:26.680 --> 00:43:28.960 align:middle line:84%
So this is formally
what a belief is.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.510 align:middle line:84%
And now, let me formally define
the function that people rightly

00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:33.810 align:middle line:90%
challenged me on a little bit.

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:36.490 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to formally
extend this function,

00:43:36.490 --> 00:43:43.310 align:middle line:84%
we're going to define ui
of Si beta negative i.

00:43:43.310 --> 00:43:47.070 align:middle line:84%
So remember, so far, we only
defined player i's utility

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:48.870 align:middle line:90%
from a strategy profile.

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to think about
what is player i's expected

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:55.950 align:middle line:84%
utility if player i plays
strategy Si and player

00:43:55.950 --> 00:44:00.270 align:middle line:84%
i holds this belief about
everyone else's strategies.

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.030 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
take expectations.

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:04.870 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to need
to do a summation.

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:07.070 align:middle line:84%
We always sum over
things we don't know.

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:09.750 align:middle line:84%
What we don't know is
how my opponents play.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:13.150 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be a
sum over s negative i in S

00:44:13.150 --> 00:44:15.150 align:middle line:90%
negative i.

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:16.830 align:middle line:90%
And we're taking an expectation.

00:44:16.830 --> 00:44:19.630 align:middle line:84%
So for each way that my
opponents could play,

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:22.390 align:middle line:84%
I want to consider the
probability that that happens.

00:44:22.390 --> 00:44:24.670 align:middle line:84%
And then I want to multiply
that by the utility

00:44:24.670 --> 00:44:26.670 align:middle line:90%
I get if that does happen.

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:31.610 align:middle line:84%
So what's the probability
that S negative i happens?

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:36.090 align:middle line:84%
It's beta negative
i of S negative i.

00:44:36.090 --> 00:44:38.050 align:middle line:84%
And I want to multiply
this by the utility

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:39.690 align:middle line:90%
I get if that happens.

00:44:39.690 --> 00:44:41.010 align:middle line:90%
Well, what's my utility?

00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:43.370 align:middle line:90%
Well, I'm still playing Si.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:46.210 align:middle line:84%
If it turns out my
opponents play S negative i,

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:52.490 align:middle line:84%
then my utility is ui
of Si, S negative i.

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:54.750 align:middle line:84%
Let me maybe draw a line
here to make it clear

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:56.810 align:middle line:90%
this is a separate line.

00:44:56.810 --> 00:44:59.050 align:middle line:84%
And this is the
formal definition--

00:44:59.050 --> 00:45:01.130 align:middle line:84%
or the formal extension
of this function

00:45:01.130 --> 00:45:04.490 align:middle line:90%
to strategies and beliefs.

00:45:04.490 --> 00:45:06.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can also see here
what we mentioned earlier.

00:45:06.810 --> 00:45:09.530 align:middle line:84%
What if beta negative
i is the special belief

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:12.210 align:middle line:84%
that puts probability 1
on a particular strategy

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.090 align:middle line:90%
of my opponents?

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:16.330 align:middle line:84%
If that happens, then
all of these terms

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:19.010 align:middle line:90%
become 0 except one of them.

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:21.970 align:middle line:84%
And I simply get the
same utility function

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:24.490 align:middle line:84%
that I started with, where
S negative i is exactly

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:29.830 align:middle line:84%
the strategy that my belief
puts probability 1 on.

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:32.810 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about this?

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:33.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:45:33.310 --> 00:45:35.410 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So in this case,
just for the left side,

00:45:35.410 --> 00:45:38.490 align:middle line:84%
we have the cross-product
of [INAUDIBLE] strategy

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:40.750 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] while the
ui on the right side

00:45:40.750 --> 00:45:42.890 align:middle line:90%
is just purely [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:42.890 --> 00:45:43.890 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:50.150 align:middle line:84%
So this lives in S.
And this lives in Si

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:53.930 align:middle line:90%
cross delta of S negative i.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:56.650 align:middle line:84%
But I don't want people
to be scared by the math.

00:45:56.650 --> 00:46:02.430 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, that's where
these things live.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:03.350 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:46:03.350 --> 00:46:05.190 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.310 align:middle line:84%
So now we've talked
about beliefs,

00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:11.150 align:middle line:84%
now we can talk about a
best response to a belief.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:14.510 align:middle line:84%
And this is, I think, one
of the most central ideas

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:16.630 align:middle line:90%
in game theory.

00:46:16.630 --> 00:46:18.130 align:middle line:84%
Mathematically,
it's not very deep.

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:21.230 align:middle line:84%
But conceptually, it's really,
I think, a step forward.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:22.730 align:middle line:84%
In interactive
decision problems,

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:24.950 align:middle line:84%
the idea is that
there's two steps.

00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:28.490 align:middle line:84%
First, I form a belief about how
my opponents are going to play.

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:30.450 align:middle line:84%
And then I choose
a strategy that's

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:33.610 align:middle line:84%
best given the belief that
I form about everyone else.

00:46:33.610 --> 00:46:36.370 align:middle line:84%
So we want to
formally define that.

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:53.770 align:middle line:84%
So we say strategy Si is a
best response to belief beta

00:46:53.770 --> 00:46:54.470 align:middle line:90%
negative.

00:46:54.470 --> 00:46:56.550 align:middle line:84%
And remember, for any
of this to make sense,

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:58.930 align:middle line:84%
we have some game
in the background.

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:00.430 align:middle line:84%
We have some game
in the background.

00:47:00.430 --> 00:47:01.428 align:middle line:90%
We have some player.

00:47:01.428 --> 00:47:02.970 align:middle line:84%
And if I wanted to
be really precise,

00:47:02.970 --> 00:47:06.650 align:middle line:84%
I would say, for
player i, strategy Si

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:09.850 align:middle line:84%
is a best response to
belief beta negative i,

00:47:09.850 --> 00:47:11.490 align:middle line:84%
because we're
evaluating things here

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:13.490 align:middle line:84%
from the perspective
of player i.

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Player i chooses a
strategy for herself

00:47:16.130 --> 00:47:20.010 align:middle line:84%
and forms beliefs about
everyone else's strategy.

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:23.010 align:middle line:90%
Well, what do we need?

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:27.240 align:middle line:84%
Well, we want Si to do better
than any other strategy given

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:28.240 align:middle line:90%
the belief.

00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:30.940 align:middle line:90%
So we need ui of Si.

00:47:30.940 --> 00:47:42.282 align:middle line:90%


00:47:42.282 --> 00:47:43.240 align:middle line:90%
So what am I going to--

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:44.520 align:middle line:90%
I'll fill it in a second.

00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:45.680 align:middle line:90%
I'm player i.

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:49.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm comparing the utility that
I get from Si to the utility

00:47:49.520 --> 00:47:51.760 align:middle line:90%
I get from Si prime.

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:56.360 align:middle line:84%
And I want Si to be better
than Si prime for any Si prime

00:47:56.360 --> 00:47:57.920 align:middle line:90%
that I contemplate.

00:47:57.920 --> 00:47:59.020 align:middle line:90%
But what goes in here?

00:47:59.020 --> 00:48:02.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, what goes in here is
the fixed belief that I have.

00:48:02.320 --> 00:48:04.320 align:middle line:90%
So this is beta negative i.

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:05.660 align:middle line:90%
And this is beta negative i.

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:10.640 align:middle line:90%


00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:13.100 align:middle line:84%
So given the belief
beta negative i,

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:17.840 align:middle line:84%
I can compute my expected
utility from any strategy.

00:48:17.840 --> 00:48:22.340 align:middle line:84%
And we say that strategy Si is
a best response to my belief

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:24.860 align:middle line:84%
if the expected
utility I get from Si

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:27.540 align:middle line:84%
is weakly larger than
the expected utility

00:48:27.540 --> 00:48:30.743 align:middle line:84%
I get from any other
strategy Si prime.

00:48:30.743 --> 00:48:33.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's go back to the
question that was asked earlier.

00:48:33.160 --> 00:48:36.580 align:middle line:84%
Can I have multiple best
responses to a belief?

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:37.660 align:middle line:90%
Is that possible?

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:41.320 align:middle line:84%
Can two strategies both be best
responses to the same belief?

00:48:41.320 --> 00:48:47.180 align:middle line:90%


00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:47.820 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:49.940 align:middle line:84%
So this goes back to
the suggestion before.

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:52.580 align:middle line:84%
If I had two strategies,
Si and Si prime,

00:48:52.580 --> 00:48:55.020 align:middle line:84%
that gave me exactly the
same utility no matter what

00:48:55.020 --> 00:48:57.140 align:middle line:84%
my opponents did, then
it's possible for them

00:48:57.140 --> 00:48:58.880 align:middle line:90%
both to be best responses.

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:01.000 align:middle line:84%
In fact, they could
both be best responses

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:03.720 align:middle line:84%
even if they didn't give me
exactly the same utility,

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:05.900 align:middle line:84%
but the expected
utility was the same.

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:10.580 align:middle line:90%
So let's note here--

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:19.240 align:middle line:84%
there can be multiple best
responses to the same belief.

00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:28.280 align:middle line:90%


00:49:28.280 --> 00:49:29.860 align:middle line:84%
If we wanted there
to only be one,

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:32.705 align:middle line:84%
we sometimes might use the
phrase strict best response

00:49:32.705 --> 00:49:35.080 align:middle line:84%
to say this is a best response
and nothing else is a best

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:36.200 align:middle line:90%
response.

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:40.000 align:middle line:84%
But we'll generally
just use the weak sense.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:40.500 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:49:40.500 --> 00:49:45.000 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to understand
this graphically.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:47.520 align:middle line:90%
I think there's space.

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:49.140 align:middle line:90%
Maybe let's do this over here.

00:49:49.140 --> 00:49:55.580 align:middle line:90%
So let me just erase this.

00:49:55.580 --> 00:49:58.240 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:00.560 align:middle line:90%


00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:01.720 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:05.560 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so delta is the space of
lotteries over the set, yeah.

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:09.517 align:middle line:90%


00:50:09.517 --> 00:50:11.600 align:middle line:84%
And let me actually just
erase this to give myself

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:12.580 align:middle line:90%
a bit more space.

00:50:12.580 --> 00:50:16.040 align:middle line:90%


00:50:16.040 --> 00:50:20.940 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to understand
best responses in this game.

00:50:20.940 --> 00:50:22.240 align:middle line:90%
Best responses to what?

00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:26.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, best responses for
player 1 to player 2's--

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:30.380 align:middle line:84%
to player 1's belief
about player 2's strategy.

00:50:30.380 --> 00:50:33.820 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to do is we're
going to draw some payoffs here.

00:50:33.820 --> 00:50:36.340 align:middle line:90%


00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:41.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to draw a graph here
where, on the horizontal axis,

00:50:41.680 --> 00:50:42.180 align:middle line:90%
I'm--

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:44.780 align:middle line:90%


00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:47.780 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's better
if I put p on R.

00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:53.800 align:middle line:84%
So let me just switch this so
that we're geometrically better.

00:50:53.800 --> 00:51:00.580 align:middle line:90%


00:51:00.580 --> 00:51:03.580 align:middle line:84%
So I can describe my belief
by my probability on L

00:51:03.580 --> 00:51:05.340 align:middle line:84%
or my probability
on R. It's more

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:07.550 align:middle line:84%
convenient to put
the probability on R

00:51:07.550 --> 00:51:09.800 align:middle line:84%
because then the graph is
going to move left to right.

00:51:09.800 --> 00:51:11.300 align:middle line:84%
And that's going
to be a bit better.

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:15.660 align:middle line:84%
So p down here is
the probability on R.

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:19.650 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to draw
this graph from 0 to 1.

00:51:19.650 --> 00:51:22.570 align:middle line:84%
So if p equals 1, then I'm
certain that my opponent is

00:51:22.570 --> 00:51:26.850 align:middle line:84%
playing R. If p equals 0, then
I'm certain that my opponent is

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:31.570 align:middle line:84%
playing L. And say if p is 1/2,
then I think it's equally likely

00:51:31.570 --> 00:51:34.310 align:middle line:84%
that my opponent plays L and
that my opponent plays R.

00:51:34.310 --> 00:51:36.750 align:middle line:84%
You can see why I switch things
so that we move from left

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:37.250 align:middle line:90%
to right.

00:51:37.250 --> 00:51:40.050 align:middle line:84%
I think it's a bit
more intuitive.

00:51:40.050 --> 00:51:43.370 align:middle line:84%
And now, to think
about best responses,

00:51:43.370 --> 00:51:46.010 align:middle line:84%
I basically want to plot
each of these functions.

00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:50.410 align:middle line:84%
I want to say, if I play T,
what is my utility from T

00:51:50.410 --> 00:51:54.250 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the belief
that I have about my opponents?

00:51:54.250 --> 00:51:57.730 align:middle line:90%
So let's start.

00:51:57.730 --> 00:52:03.450 align:middle line:90%
Let's put negative 1, 0, 1, 2.

00:52:03.450 --> 00:52:07.150 align:middle line:84%
If I play T, well, if
my opponent plays L,

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:09.010 align:middle line:90%
I get a payoff of 2.

00:52:09.010 --> 00:52:11.210 align:middle line:84%
So that means, if I'm
certain that my opponent is

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:13.630 align:middle line:84%
going to play L, if
that's my belief,

00:52:13.630 --> 00:52:15.390 align:middle line:90%
then my payoff better be 2.

00:52:15.390 --> 00:52:18.150 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
get a point up here.

00:52:18.150 --> 00:52:20.630 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm certain that
my opponent is playing R,

00:52:20.630 --> 00:52:22.910 align:middle line:84%
then I get a payoff
of negative 1.

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:27.910 align:middle line:84%
So it better be that we
get a point like this.

00:52:27.910 --> 00:52:29.890 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out-- we
could calculate it.

00:52:29.890 --> 00:52:31.830 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out expected
utilities are always

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:33.190 align:middle line:90%
linear in my beliefs.

00:52:33.190 --> 00:52:36.710 align:middle line:84%
So once we know these two
points, we can just fill it in.

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.110 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
a line like this.

00:52:40.110 --> 00:52:46.532 align:middle line:90%
So this is u1 of T, p.

00:52:46.532 --> 00:52:48.990 align:middle line:84%
And remember, if you did the
formula earlier in your notes,

00:52:48.990 --> 00:52:52.030 align:middle line:90%
we've switched p to 1 minus p.

00:52:52.030 --> 00:52:55.070 align:middle line:84%
So the formula in your notes
was for the old convention.

00:52:55.070 --> 00:52:58.390 align:middle line:90%
So that no longer applies.

00:52:58.390 --> 00:53:00.710 align:middle line:90%
What about B?

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:02.450 align:middle line:90%
Well, B is symmetric.

00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:05.550 align:middle line:84%
If I play B, then if I know
my opponent is playing left,

00:53:05.550 --> 00:53:08.310 align:middle line:90%
I get negative 1.

00:53:08.310 --> 00:53:12.143 align:middle line:84%
And if I know my opponent
is playing right, I get 2.

00:53:12.143 --> 00:53:15.290 align:middle line:84%
Let me test my
drawing skills here.

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:18.130 align:middle line:90%
And it's going to be linear.

00:53:18.130 --> 00:53:21.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get
something like this.

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:21.760 align:middle line:90%
What about M?

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:23.510 align:middle line:84%
What is the graph of
M going to look like?

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:27.050 align:middle line:84%
It's going to look a
bit different, right?

00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:28.750 align:middle line:90%
What will that line look like?

00:53:28.750 --> 00:53:29.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:29.590 --> 00:53:30.600 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:32.350 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It's just a
horizontal line at 0

00:53:32.350 --> 00:53:34.950 align:middle line:84%
because, if I play M,
whatever my opponent does,

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:36.160 align:middle line:90%
I get a payoff of 0.

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:37.910 align:middle line:84%
So whatever I believe
my opponent's doing,

00:53:37.910 --> 00:53:39.730 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to get a payoff of 0.

00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:43.330 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have a
horizontal line here at 0.

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:48.090 align:middle line:84%
And if we're careful, this
intersection should be at 1/2.

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:50.690 align:middle line:90%
You can check that.

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:52.830 align:middle line:84%
So let me finish
writing these out.

00:53:52.830 --> 00:54:03.370 align:middle line:90%


00:54:03.370 --> 00:54:06.330 align:middle line:84%
So to be clear, each of these
is a different function of p.

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:08.270 align:middle line:84%
We have three different
functions of p,

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:10.370 align:middle line:84%
where p is a number
between 0 and 1.

00:54:10.370 --> 00:54:13.520 align:middle line:84%
And I've graphed all three
of the functions here.

00:54:13.520 --> 00:54:15.440 align:middle line:84%
So from the graph, we
should be able to say

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:18.000 align:middle line:90%
a lot about best responses.

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:21.180 align:middle line:84%
Can anyone read off from the
graph what my best response is?

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:25.200 align:middle line:90%


00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:26.800 align:middle line:90%
Let's go through it, right?

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:28.600 align:middle line:90%
Let's fix a belief.

00:54:28.600 --> 00:54:31.440 align:middle line:84%
A belief is some point
on the horizontal axis.

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:33.000 align:middle line:84%
Now, once I fix
that belief, if I

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:36.060 align:middle line:84%
want to understand my payoff
from my different strategies,

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:40.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to draw a
vertical line upwards.

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:42.380 align:middle line:84%
And as I pass up through
that vertical line,

00:54:42.380 --> 00:54:46.520 align:middle line:84%
each curve I intersect is
telling me my expected payoff

00:54:46.520 --> 00:54:49.743 align:middle line:84%
from that particular
strategy given this belief.

00:54:49.743 --> 00:54:51.160 align:middle line:84%
So at this point,
what it tells me

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:56.680 align:middle line:84%
is my lowest payoff is from B.
M gives me my middle payoff.

00:54:56.680 --> 00:55:01.360 align:middle line:84%
And then T gives me
the highest payoff.

00:55:01.360 --> 00:55:05.440 align:middle line:84%
So at any beliefs where
the highest curve is T,

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:07.720 align:middle line:90%
T is my best response.

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:10.280 align:middle line:84%
At any beliefs where B
is the highest curve,

00:55:10.280 --> 00:55:12.100 align:middle line:90%
B is my best response.

00:55:12.100 --> 00:55:14.100 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at this.

00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:16.200 align:middle line:84%
I should have gotten
out the colored chalk.

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:19.140 align:middle line:90%


00:55:19.140 --> 00:55:20.800 align:middle line:90%
That'd be nice if I have it.

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:23.820 align:middle line:90%


00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:26.240 align:middle line:90%
Maybe no colored chalk today.

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:27.100 align:middle line:90%
Ah, yes.

00:55:27.100 --> 00:55:30.883 align:middle line:90%


00:55:30.883 --> 00:55:33.300 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm actually going to
do-- the easiest thing to do

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:35.460 align:middle line:84%
is I'm going to shade
in what's sometimes

00:55:35.460 --> 00:55:36.680 align:middle line:90%
called the upper envelope.

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:37.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go through
all these curves.

00:55:37.820 --> 00:55:39.620 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to look
at what's highest.

00:55:39.620 --> 00:55:42.590 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm here, this
is the highest curve.

00:55:42.590 --> 00:55:44.220 align:middle line:90%
It comes down.

00:55:44.220 --> 00:55:45.120 align:middle line:90%
Here, it changes.

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:48.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, this becomes
the highest curve.

00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:51.900 align:middle line:90%
And I get something like this.

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:55.700 align:middle line:90%
So now, you can see down here--

00:55:55.700 --> 00:55:59.380 align:middle line:84%
can anyone now read off from the
graph what my best responses are

00:55:59.380 --> 00:56:01.420 align:middle line:90%
as a function of my beliefs?

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:03.660 align:middle line:90%
So when is T a best response?

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:05.900 align:middle line:84%
For which beliefs is
T a best response?

00:56:05.900 --> 00:56:06.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:08.880 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: From 0 to 1/2
probability of choosing R.

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:12.860 align:middle line:84%
And then from 1/2 to 1, it's
better to use B instead.

00:56:12.860 --> 00:56:14.860 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:14.860 --> 00:56:16.380 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Great,
so exactly right.

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:18.940 align:middle line:84%
And a key point to make at
the end, these are inclusive.

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So at 1/2, I actually
have two best responses.

00:56:20.960 --> 00:56:24.320 align:middle line:84%
T and B are both best responses
at 1/2, as you pointed out.

00:56:24.320 --> 00:56:27.800 align:middle line:84%
And if my belief is below
1/2, then T is the unique best

00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:30.080 align:middle line:84%
response, or the
strict best response.

00:56:30.080 --> 00:56:33.640 align:middle line:84%
And if p is above 1/2, then
B is the unique best response

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:36.540 align:middle line:90%
or the strict best response.

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:37.040 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:41.000 align:middle line:84%
Does this make sense/ Well,
yes, because T does really well

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:43.240 align:middle line:90%
if my opponent plays left.

00:56:43.240 --> 00:56:45.823 align:middle line:84%
So if my probability on
right is small enough

00:56:45.823 --> 00:56:48.240 align:middle line:84%
and I think it's likely enough
that my opponent is playing

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:51.120 align:middle line:90%
left, then T is the best option.

00:56:51.120 --> 00:56:53.960 align:middle line:84%
If it's likely enough that
my opponent is playing R,

00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:55.560 align:middle line:90%
then B is the best option.

00:56:55.560 --> 00:56:59.840 align:middle line:84%
And we see that B is the best
response-- is a best response.

00:56:59.840 --> 00:57:04.680 align:middle line:84%
But now we have a predicament,
which is, what about M?

00:57:04.680 --> 00:57:09.620 align:middle line:90%


00:57:09.620 --> 00:57:12.180 align:middle line:84%
M is not a best
response to any belief.

00:57:12.180 --> 00:57:13.640 align:middle line:84%
Does this mean we
shouldn't play M?

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:15.740 align:middle line:90%
What do you think about M?

00:57:15.740 --> 00:57:16.840 align:middle line:90%
Would you ever play it?

00:57:16.840 --> 00:57:20.660 align:middle line:90%


00:57:20.660 --> 00:57:21.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:23.940 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If you're super
uncertain about your beliefs

00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:27.780 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE], you
might consider M.

00:57:27.780 --> 00:57:31.305 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: We have to be really
careful with those words.

00:57:31.305 --> 00:57:33.180 align:middle line:84%
What you're describing
is something important

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:34.460 align:middle line:84%
that we're not going to
cover in this class, which

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:35.680 align:middle line:90%
is called ambiguity aversion.

00:57:35.680 --> 00:57:40.360 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want to go
too far down this path.

00:57:40.360 --> 00:57:42.640 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to stick
with expected utility.

00:57:42.640 --> 00:57:44.452 align:middle line:84%
And risk aversion
would not actually

00:57:44.452 --> 00:57:47.160 align:middle line:84%
cause you to choose M. Something
called ambiguity aversion would.

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:48.785 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going
to talk about that.

00:57:48.785 --> 00:57:51.420 align:middle line:84%
So we're imagining that we
have our beliefs well-defined

00:57:51.420 --> 00:57:53.620 align:middle line:84%
about how the other
person is doing.

00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:55.860 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's actually
fair to say I don't really

00:57:55.860 --> 00:57:59.780 align:middle line:84%
want to play M. There's no
belief against which M is a best

00:57:59.780 --> 00:58:00.340 align:middle line:90%
response.

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:04.850 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's natural
to think back to dominance.

00:58:04.850 --> 00:58:07.450 align:middle line:84%
What we said before is you might
want to throw out a strategy

00:58:07.450 --> 00:58:09.530 align:middle line:84%
if it's dominated
by another strategy.

00:58:09.530 --> 00:58:12.130 align:middle line:84%
But now, we're in this
weird middle ground, where

00:58:12.130 --> 00:58:15.610 align:middle line:84%
strategy M is not a best
response to any belief,

00:58:15.610 --> 00:58:18.810 align:middle line:84%
but it's also not dominated
by any other strategy.

00:58:18.810 --> 00:58:21.330 align:middle line:90%
And that is a weird conundrum.

00:58:21.330 --> 00:58:23.682 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to show that,
in fact, M is dominated.

00:58:23.682 --> 00:58:25.390 align:middle line:84%
We just have to be a
little more careful.

00:58:25.390 --> 00:58:28.893 align:middle line:84%
Can anyone see a sense
in which M is dominated?

00:58:28.893 --> 00:58:30.810 align:middle line:84%
Is there anything I could
do that would always

00:58:30.810 --> 00:58:32.390 align:middle line:90%
do better than M?

00:58:32.390 --> 00:58:32.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:35.290 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
strategy that basically

00:58:35.290 --> 00:58:37.250 align:middle line:90%
varies on whatever [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:37.250 --> 00:58:41.210 align:middle line:90%


00:58:41.210 --> 00:58:43.647 align:middle line:84%
otherwise, that
strategy [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:43.647 --> 00:58:45.730 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: OK, so I like
the first part of what you

00:58:45.730 --> 00:58:46.870 align:middle line:90%
said about a mixed strategy.

00:58:46.870 --> 00:58:48.412 align:middle line:84%
But crucially, with
a mixed strategy,

00:58:48.412 --> 00:58:52.270 align:middle line:84%
we can't vary our mixing
probability based on our belief.

00:58:52.270 --> 00:58:53.590 align:middle line:90%
But the mixed idea is crucial.

00:58:53.590 --> 00:58:56.908 align:middle line:84%
So what if I said, instead
of choosing strategy M,

00:58:56.908 --> 00:58:57.950 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to flip a coin.

00:58:57.950 --> 00:58:59.050 align:middle line:90%
Do you want to add something?

00:58:59.050 --> 00:58:59.550 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:00.092 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:59:00.092 --> 00:59:01.710 align:middle line:90%
Imagine I flipped a coin.

00:59:01.710 --> 00:59:04.750 align:middle line:84%
And if the coin is heads,
I'm going to play T.

00:59:04.750 --> 00:59:09.917 align:middle line:84%
So consider the strategy which
we'll call 1/2 T plus 1/2 B.

00:59:09.917 --> 00:59:10.750 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean?

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:12.118 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to flip a coin.

00:59:12.118 --> 00:59:13.910 align:middle line:84%
If it's heads, I play--
well, if it's heads

00:59:13.910 --> 00:59:15.118 align:middle line:90%
I play T. That's a bit weird.

00:59:15.118 --> 00:59:17.470 align:middle line:84%
If it's tails I play T.
If it's heads, I play B.

00:59:17.470 --> 00:59:20.910 align:middle line:90%
If I were to do that--

00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:22.970 align:middle line:84%
let's add this
strategy down here.

00:59:22.970 --> 00:59:32.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is 1/2 T plus 1/2
B. If my opponent plays L,

00:59:32.350 --> 00:59:35.470 align:middle line:84%
what is my payoff from this
mixed strategy where I flip

00:59:35.470 --> 00:59:36.010 align:middle line:90%
a coin?

00:59:36.010 --> 00:59:41.470 align:middle line:90%


00:59:41.470 --> 00:59:42.790 align:middle line:90%
What's my expected payoff?

00:59:42.790 --> 00:59:48.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, with probability
1/2 I play T and I get 2.

00:59:48.750 --> 00:59:51.910 align:middle line:84%
With probability 1/2 I play
B and I get negative 1.

00:59:51.910 --> 00:59:55.430 align:middle line:84%
So my expected payoff
is 1/2 of 2, which is 1,

00:59:55.430 --> 00:59:57.850 align:middle line:84%
plus 1/2 negative 1,
which is minus 1/2.

00:59:57.850 --> 01:00:01.930 align:middle line:90%
And if I add it up, I get 1/2.

01:00:01.930 --> 01:00:05.730 align:middle line:90%
And now, let's go to this side.

01:00:05.730 --> 01:00:09.290 align:middle line:84%
If I do my same coin flip,
with probability 1/2,

01:00:09.290 --> 01:00:11.370 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to get negative 1.

01:00:11.370 --> 01:00:14.290 align:middle line:84%
With probability 1/2,
I'm going to get 2.

01:00:14.290 --> 01:00:16.190 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll do
the addition here.

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:21.750 align:middle line:84%
It's 1/2 times 2 plus
1/2 times negative 1.

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:24.750 align:middle line:84%
It's easier to distribute and
say, well, 2 minus 1 is 1.

01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:26.490 align:middle line:84%
So it's 1/2 times
1, which is 1/2.

01:00:26.490 --> 01:00:31.130 align:middle line:90%


01:00:31.130 --> 01:00:34.850 align:middle line:90%
Well, now, is M dominated?

01:00:34.850 --> 01:00:37.530 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, we
see that M is not

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:39.730 align:middle line:90%
dominated by any pure strategy.

01:00:39.730 --> 01:00:42.330 align:middle line:84%
But it is dominated by what's
called a mixed strategy.

01:00:42.330 --> 01:00:45.490 align:middle line:84%
There's a way I can
flip a coin so that,

01:00:45.490 --> 01:00:49.410 align:middle line:84%
if I flip a coin in this way,
well, if my opponent plays L,

01:00:49.410 --> 01:00:52.570 align:middle line:84%
I get 1/2 from this mixed
strategy and I only get 0 from

01:00:52.570 --> 01:00:53.930 align:middle line:90%
m.

01:00:53.930 --> 01:00:57.680 align:middle line:84%
And if my opponent plays R, I
get 1/2 from this mixed strategy

01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:59.920 align:middle line:90%
and I only get 0 from M.

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:03.360 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, whatever belief I
have about however my opponents

01:01:03.360 --> 01:01:05.933 align:middle line:84%
play, I do strictly
better under this mixture.

01:01:05.933 --> 01:01:08.100 align:middle line:84%
We could even write down
the payoff of this mixture.

01:01:08.100 --> 01:01:10.600 align:middle line:84%
It's actually going to
look exactly like this.

01:01:10.600 --> 01:01:13.640 align:middle line:84%
If we wanted to say, what
is my expected payoff

01:01:13.640 --> 01:01:16.633 align:middle line:84%
from this mixed strategy
as a function of my belief?

01:01:16.633 --> 01:01:18.800 align:middle line:84%
So what I have here-- let
me put it a bit separate--

01:01:18.800 --> 01:01:25.200 align:middle line:84%
is u1 of 1/2 T
plus 1/2 B comma p.

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:28.600 align:middle line:84%
So this says, if I believe my
opponent is playing right with

01:01:28.600 --> 01:01:33.520 align:middle line:84%
probability p and I flip a coin
and play T with probability 1/2

01:01:33.520 --> 01:01:36.280 align:middle line:84%
and B with probability 1/2,
what is my payoff going to be?

01:01:36.280 --> 01:01:39.240 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the dominance
relation because this is always

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:41.760 align:middle line:90%
higher than this.

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:44.080 align:middle line:84%
So do we think people
really play mixed strategies

01:01:44.080 --> 01:01:45.360 align:middle line:90%
in practice?

01:01:45.360 --> 01:01:46.450 align:middle line:90%
We can debate about it.

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:48.200 align:middle line:84%
But I want to point
out that this resolves

01:01:48.200 --> 01:01:51.260 align:middle line:84%
this seeming paradox at
first where, on the one hand,

01:01:51.260 --> 01:01:53.620 align:middle line:84%
M was never a best
response to any belief.

01:01:53.620 --> 01:01:55.600 align:middle line:84%
So it seemed like a
really bad strategy.

01:01:55.600 --> 01:01:57.340 align:middle line:84%
But we couldn't
directly show what

01:01:57.340 --> 01:01:58.900 align:middle line:90%
would always be better than it.

01:01:58.900 --> 01:02:00.720 align:middle line:84%
By using mixed
strategies, we can.

01:02:00.720 --> 01:02:03.260 align:middle line:84%
We see that, in
fact, M is dominated.

01:02:03.260 --> 01:02:05.080 align:middle line:84%
It's, in fact,
strictly dominated,

01:02:05.080 --> 01:02:06.760 align:middle line:90%
just not by a pure strategy.

01:02:06.760 --> 01:02:08.940 align:middle line:84%
It's strictly dominated
by a mixed strategy.

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:11.060 align:middle line:84%
So let's formally
define a mixed strategy.

01:02:11.060 --> 01:02:14.800 align:middle line:84%
It's what you would think,
but be a little careful.

01:02:14.800 --> 01:02:26.300 align:middle line:90%


01:02:26.300 --> 01:02:28.020 align:middle line:90%
So what is a mixed strategy?

01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:30.560 align:middle line:84%
It says, well,
just like a belief,

01:02:30.560 --> 01:02:32.980 align:middle line:84%
it's a probability
distribution, or a lottery.

01:02:32.980 --> 01:02:35.520 align:middle line:84%
But it's not a lottery about
other people's strategies.

01:02:35.520 --> 01:02:37.360 align:middle line:84%
It's a lottery about
my own strategy.

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:45.470 align:middle line:84%
So a mixed strategy for
player i is a function--

01:02:45.470 --> 01:02:47.600 align:middle line:84%
or is a probability
distribution.

01:02:47.600 --> 01:02:50.540 align:middle line:90%


01:02:50.540 --> 01:02:53.320 align:middle line:84%
We often use the
notation sigma i.

01:02:53.320 --> 01:02:55.840 align:middle line:84%
So we use Si for
a pure strategy.

01:02:55.840 --> 01:02:58.180 align:middle line:84%
And sigma i, it's like
the Greek version of S.

01:02:58.180 --> 01:02:59.920 align:middle line:84%
We use that for
a mixed strategy.

01:02:59.920 --> 01:03:02.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, now, it's going
to be a function from Si

01:03:02.800 --> 01:03:08.840 align:middle line:84%
to 0, 1 that says, for each
possible strategy, what

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:11.277 align:middle line:84%
is the probability that
I play that strategy?

01:03:11.277 --> 01:03:13.360 align:middle line:84%
But once again, we need
another condition for this

01:03:13.360 --> 01:03:14.080 align:middle line:90%
to make sense.

01:03:14.080 --> 01:03:16.440 align:middle line:84%
The probabilities
have to sum up to 1.

01:03:16.440 --> 01:03:18.500 align:middle line:90%
So it's a function satisfying--

01:03:18.500 --> 01:03:21.320 align:middle line:90%


01:03:21.320 --> 01:03:26.120 align:middle line:84%
well, if I sum over
all my strategies,

01:03:26.120 --> 01:03:29.500 align:middle line:84%
the probability I put on that
strategy, that better sum to 1.

01:03:29.500 --> 01:03:35.240 align:middle line:90%


01:03:35.240 --> 01:03:37.960 align:middle line:84%
And I asked before, do people
ever play mixed strategies?

01:03:37.960 --> 01:03:39.940 align:middle line:84%
In a lot of economic
contexts, they don't.

01:03:39.940 --> 01:03:41.482 align:middle line:84%
But there's actually
one domain where

01:03:41.482 --> 01:03:43.100 align:middle line:90%
mixed strategies are crucial.

01:03:43.100 --> 01:03:45.920 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone know
what's a domain where

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:49.100 align:middle line:84%
people are very careful
to play mixed strategies?

01:03:49.100 --> 01:03:51.947 align:middle line:90%


01:03:51.947 --> 01:03:54.280 align:middle line:84%
Or are there times where
you've played a mixed strategy?

01:03:54.280 --> 01:03:57.320 align:middle line:90%


01:03:57.320 --> 01:03:57.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:57.820 --> 01:03:59.112 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Rock, paper, scissors.

01:03:59.112 --> 01:04:01.340 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Rock, paper,
scissors is a good one.

01:04:01.340 --> 01:04:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And if you played in rock,
paper, scissors in competitions,

01:04:03.320 --> 01:04:04.760 align:middle line:84%
yeah, that would probably
be really important.

01:04:04.760 --> 01:04:06.460 align:middle line:84%
There's another domain where
people make a lot of money

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:08.460 align:middle line:84%
where randomization
is also important.

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:09.020 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:04:09.020 --> 01:04:09.480 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Poker.

01:04:09.480 --> 01:04:10.688 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Professional poker.

01:04:10.688 --> 01:04:12.540 align:middle line:84%
So professional poker
players are extremely

01:04:12.540 --> 01:04:13.457 align:middle line:90%
good at randomization.

01:04:13.457 --> 01:04:15.325 align:middle line:84%
And they train to
randomize well.

01:04:15.325 --> 01:04:17.200 align:middle line:84%
Humans are very bad at
randomizing naturally.

01:04:17.200 --> 01:04:19.380 align:middle line:84%
And there's all these techniques
professional poker players make.

01:04:19.380 --> 01:04:21.703 align:middle line:84%
15 years ago, you could
play poker and win money

01:04:21.703 --> 01:04:23.120 align:middle line:84%
without being good
at randomizing.

01:04:23.120 --> 01:04:24.602 align:middle line:84%
Today, you would
just get destroyed

01:04:24.602 --> 01:04:27.060 align:middle line:84%
because the best players are
extremely good at randomizing.

01:04:27.060 --> 01:04:29.060 align:middle line:90%
And they study game theory.

01:04:29.060 --> 01:04:31.540 align:middle line:84%
And they randomize
extremely carefully.

01:04:31.540 --> 01:04:33.043 align:middle line:84%
And basically,
the people who are

01:04:33.043 --> 01:04:35.460 align:middle line:84%
better at randomizing with
exactly the right probabilities

01:04:35.460 --> 01:04:36.500 align:middle line:90%
win a lot of money.

01:04:36.500 --> 01:04:38.740 align:middle line:84%
So poker is a great
example of mixed strategies

01:04:38.740 --> 01:04:39.538 align:middle line:90%
that really happen.

01:04:39.538 --> 01:04:41.080 align:middle line:84%
If I taught this
course 20 years ago,

01:04:41.080 --> 01:04:43.300 align:middle line:84%
it would be hard to come up
with an example of people

01:04:43.300 --> 01:04:44.360 align:middle line:90%
playing mixed strategies.

01:04:44.360 --> 01:04:47.277 align:middle line:84%
And now, the reality has
caught up to the game theory.

01:04:47.277 --> 01:04:49.610 align:middle line:84%
And now, there's a lot of
people who make a lot of money

01:04:49.610 --> 01:04:51.770 align:middle line:90%
by mixing.

01:04:51.770 --> 01:04:52.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:52.270 --> 01:04:55.450 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's move
on a little bit

01:04:55.450 --> 01:04:57.990 align:middle line:84%
and try to understand how
general this phenomenon is.

01:04:57.990 --> 01:05:00.290 align:middle line:84%
So I want to make
one observation here.

01:05:00.290 --> 01:05:02.730 align:middle line:84%
And then we want to try to
see how general that is.

01:05:02.730 --> 01:05:06.930 align:middle line:84%
So if you're approaching a game
like this and you're player 1,

01:05:06.930 --> 01:05:08.830 align:middle line:84%
there's two ways you
could go about it.

01:05:08.830 --> 01:05:12.410 align:middle line:84%
If I were to ask you what is
a reasonable strategy to play,

01:05:12.410 --> 01:05:14.330 align:middle line:90%
you could say, well--

01:05:14.330 --> 01:05:18.690 align:middle line:84%
so let me say, let's
look at player 1.

01:05:18.690 --> 01:05:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just say,
which strategies?

01:05:20.430 --> 01:05:23.902 align:middle line:90%


01:05:23.902 --> 01:05:25.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, one thing you
could do is you could

01:05:25.610 --> 01:05:27.610 align:middle line:90%
start with the good strategies.

01:05:27.610 --> 01:05:30.110 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll call them
the reasonable strategies.

01:05:30.110 --> 01:05:33.935 align:middle line:90%


01:05:33.935 --> 01:05:35.310 align:middle line:84%
This is not really
a formal term.

01:05:35.310 --> 01:05:37.730 align:middle line:84%
But I'll just say
reasonable strategies.

01:05:37.730 --> 01:05:39.250 align:middle line:84%
And these would
be the strategies

01:05:39.250 --> 01:05:41.590 align:middle line:84%
that are best responses
to some belief.

01:05:41.590 --> 01:05:52.043 align:middle line:90%


01:05:52.043 --> 01:05:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want you to think too
much, for the moment, about how

01:05:54.710 --> 01:05:55.650 align:middle line:90%
you form your beliefs.

01:05:55.650 --> 01:05:58.630 align:middle line:84%
But let's just say,
is there some belief

01:05:58.630 --> 01:06:00.870 align:middle line:84%
I could have that would
make this a best response?

01:06:00.870 --> 01:06:02.895 align:middle line:84%
So in this game,
we saw in the graph

01:06:02.895 --> 01:06:04.270 align:middle line:84%
which strategies
in this game are

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:06.050 align:middle line:90%
best responses to some belief.

01:06:06.050 --> 01:06:08.290 align:middle line:84%
So which strategies are
reasonable in this sense?

01:06:08.290 --> 01:06:11.630 align:middle line:90%


01:06:11.630 --> 01:06:12.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:06:12.910 --> 01:06:13.830 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: T and B.

01:06:13.830 --> 01:06:16.190 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: T and B, right?

01:06:16.190 --> 01:06:18.830 align:middle line:84%
So I might say, look, I
have three strategies.

01:06:18.830 --> 01:06:21.903 align:middle line:84%
But the reasonable ones
are T and B. Another way

01:06:21.903 --> 01:06:24.070 align:middle line:84%
you could approach it,
though, is instead of looking

01:06:24.070 --> 01:06:25.430 align:middle line:84%
for what you should
do, you could

01:06:25.430 --> 01:06:27.770 align:middle line:84%
try to look for what you don't
want to do and avoid that.

01:06:27.770 --> 01:06:30.010 align:middle line:84%
So I could say, what are
the unreasonable strategies?

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:35.030 align:middle line:90%


01:06:35.030 --> 01:06:37.150 align:middle line:84%
And by an unreasonable
strategy, I

01:06:37.150 --> 01:06:40.390 align:middle line:84%
mean a strategy that's
strictly dominated

01:06:40.390 --> 01:06:42.970 align:middle line:84%
by some other strategy,
possibly mixed.

01:06:42.970 --> 01:07:00.770 align:middle line:90%


01:07:00.770 --> 01:07:04.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, technically, I only defined
dominance for pure strategies.

01:07:04.550 --> 01:07:06.090 align:middle line:84%
But everything makes
sense if I just

01:07:06.090 --> 01:07:09.610 align:middle line:84%
replace the Si's with
the sigma i's and I

01:07:09.610 --> 01:07:13.060 align:middle line:84%
interpret these utilities
as expected utilities.

01:07:13.060 --> 01:07:15.310 align:middle line:84%
So in this game, what are
the unreasonable strategies?

01:07:15.310 --> 01:07:16.810 align:middle line:84%
What are the strategies
I could throw out

01:07:16.810 --> 01:07:18.750 align:middle line:84%
by saying they're dominated
by something else?

01:07:18.750 --> 01:07:21.450 align:middle line:90%


01:07:21.450 --> 01:07:22.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:07:22.310 --> 01:07:22.890 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: M.

01:07:22.890 --> 01:07:25.180 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Just M, right?

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:26.930 align:middle line:84%
So notice here, there's
two different ways

01:07:26.930 --> 01:07:28.450 align:middle line:90%
to get to the same answer.

01:07:28.450 --> 01:07:31.090 align:middle line:84%
I could say, let
me only consider

01:07:31.090 --> 01:07:33.970 align:middle line:84%
strategies that are best
responses to some beliefs.

01:07:33.970 --> 01:07:35.110 align:middle line:90%
I would go through.

01:07:35.110 --> 01:07:38.390 align:middle line:84%
And I would just get T and
B. Or I could do the inverse.

01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:41.050 align:middle line:84%
I could say, let me throw
out all the strategies that

01:07:41.050 --> 01:07:43.870 align:middle line:84%
are dominated, that are
strictly dominated by something.

01:07:43.870 --> 01:07:48.590 align:middle line:84%
I would throw out M. And I'd
be left with just T and B.

01:07:48.590 --> 01:07:50.450 align:middle line:84%
And the question is,
is this a coincidence?

01:07:50.450 --> 01:07:52.310 align:middle line:84%
Is this a special
property of this game?

01:07:52.310 --> 01:07:54.550 align:middle line:84%
Or is this more generally
true, that we can always

01:07:54.550 --> 01:07:57.310 align:middle line:84%
split our strategies
into two groups, those

01:07:57.310 --> 01:07:59.950 align:middle line:84%
that are best responses
to some belief and those

01:07:59.950 --> 01:08:03.990 align:middle line:84%
that are strictly dominated
by some mixed strategy?

01:08:03.990 --> 01:08:06.130 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out this is
a very general property.

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:10.150 align:middle line:84%
And that'll be the first theorem
that we state in this course.

01:08:10.150 --> 01:08:12.690 align:middle line:90%
So let me state the theorem.

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:30.990 align:middle line:90%


01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:34.029 align:middle line:90%
So here's our theorem.

01:08:34.029 --> 01:08:35.170 align:middle line:90%
We need some assumptions.

01:08:35.170 --> 01:08:37.529 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we're in a
finite strategic form game.

01:08:37.529 --> 01:08:40.580 align:middle line:90%


01:08:40.580 --> 01:08:43.380 align:middle line:84%
Finite just means the set of
strategies for every player

01:08:43.380 --> 01:08:44.100 align:middle line:90%
is finite.

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:45.520 align:middle line:84%
And there's finitely
many players.

01:08:45.520 --> 01:08:58.220 align:middle line:90%


01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:03.420 align:middle line:84%
So for each player i
and each strategy Si--

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:11.747 align:middle line:90%


01:09:11.747 --> 01:09:13.580 align:middle line:84%
so before I write it,
let's just understand.

01:09:13.580 --> 01:09:17.620 align:middle line:84%
We fixed our finite
strategic form game.

01:09:17.620 --> 01:09:19.899 align:middle line:84%
We're considering
a particular player

01:09:19.899 --> 01:09:23.939 align:middle line:90%
i and a particular strategy Si.

01:09:23.939 --> 01:09:26.540 align:middle line:84%
And what we want to argue,
what we want to say,

01:09:26.540 --> 01:09:29.859 align:middle line:84%
is that this strategy falls
in exactly one of these two

01:09:29.859 --> 01:09:31.859 align:middle line:90%
categories.

01:09:31.859 --> 01:09:33.500 align:middle line:90%
It can't be in both.

01:09:33.500 --> 01:09:35.460 align:middle line:90%
And it can't be in Neither.

01:09:35.460 --> 01:09:39.140 align:middle line:84%
So what we say is exactly
one of the following holds.

01:09:39.140 --> 01:09:42.000 align:middle line:90%


01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:44.979 align:middle line:84%
Exactly one means not
both and not neither.

01:09:44.979 --> 01:09:57.120 align:middle line:90%


01:09:57.120 --> 01:10:01.840 align:middle line:84%
And one is that this strategy
Si is reasonable in this sense.

01:10:01.840 --> 01:10:04.780 align:middle line:84%
Meaning what/ Si is a best
response to some belief.

01:10:04.780 --> 01:10:17.975 align:middle line:90%


01:10:17.975 --> 01:10:20.100 align:middle line:84%
And when I say belief, what
is that a belief about?

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:25.140 align:middle line:84%
It's a belief-- maybe I'll
say over S negative i.

01:10:25.140 --> 01:10:27.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's remind ourselves,
when player i forms beliefs,

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:29.400 align:middle line:84%
they form beliefs
over the strategy

01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:32.200 align:middle line:90%
profiles of the other players.

01:10:32.200 --> 01:10:35.420 align:middle line:84%
And then the other case is
Si is strictly dominated.

01:10:35.420 --> 01:10:43.260 align:middle line:90%


01:10:43.260 --> 01:10:44.180 align:middle line:90%
By what?

01:10:44.180 --> 01:10:46.280 align:middle line:90%
Well, by some mixed strategy.

01:10:46.280 --> 01:10:47.700 align:middle line:90%
And we have to allow mixed.

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:49.287 align:middle line:84%
If we only allowed
pure strategies,

01:10:49.287 --> 01:10:51.620 align:middle line:84%
we already saw up here that
the theorem wouldn't be true

01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:54.700 align:middle line:84%
because there are strategies
that are never best responses,

01:10:54.700 --> 01:10:57.000 align:middle line:84%
but are not dominated
by any pure strategy.

01:10:57.000 --> 01:10:59.060 align:middle line:84%
So we have to allow
for mixed strategies.

01:10:59.060 --> 01:11:01.960 align:middle line:84%
Si is strictly dominated
by some mixed strategy.

01:11:01.960 --> 01:11:09.140 align:middle line:90%


01:11:09.140 --> 01:11:11.020 align:middle line:84%
And of course, when
I say strategy,

01:11:11.020 --> 01:11:13.260 align:middle line:84%
that has to be a
strategy of player i

01:11:13.260 --> 01:11:17.180 align:middle line:84%
because player i compares
two different strategies when

01:11:17.180 --> 01:11:18.880 align:middle line:90%
they look at strict dominance.

01:11:18.880 --> 01:11:21.140 align:middle line:84%
But wait a second, I said
Si is strictly dominated

01:11:21.140 --> 01:11:22.360 align:middle line:90%
by some mixed strategy.

01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:25.980 align:middle line:90%
What about pure strategies?

01:11:25.980 --> 01:11:27.040 align:middle line:90%
Am I including those?

01:11:27.040 --> 01:11:28.060 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:28.060 --> 01:11:29.433 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Why?

01:11:29.433 --> 01:11:31.220 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:31.220 --> 01:11:35.150 align:middle line:90%


01:11:35.150 --> 01:11:36.150 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:39.450 align:middle line:84%
So every pure strategy
is a mixed strategy.

01:11:39.450 --> 01:11:42.770 align:middle line:84%
But not every mixed
strategy is a pure strategy.

01:11:42.770 --> 01:11:45.150 align:middle line:84%
So as long as I cover mixed
strategies-- in particular,

01:11:45.150 --> 01:11:47.730 align:middle line:84%
I'm including the mixtures
that put all the probability

01:11:47.730 --> 01:11:51.450 align:middle line:84%
on a single strategy, which
are simply pure strategies.

01:11:51.450 --> 01:11:53.910 align:middle line:84%
So if we want to say
that I'm not doing that,

01:11:53.910 --> 01:11:57.490 align:middle line:84%
sometimes we say
properly mixed strategy

01:11:57.490 --> 01:11:59.190 align:middle line:90%
or nonpure mixed strategy.

01:11:59.190 --> 01:12:02.710 align:middle line:84%
But here, we're allowing all
kinds of mixed strategies,

01:12:02.710 --> 01:12:05.470 align:middle line:84%
including the ones that are just
pure strategies in disguise.

01:12:05.470 --> 01:12:09.370 align:middle line:90%


01:12:09.370 --> 01:12:11.930 align:middle line:84%
And again, I think the
interpretation of this

01:12:11.930 --> 01:12:15.610 align:middle line:84%
is that there are two very
natural ways of thinking

01:12:15.610 --> 01:12:18.390 align:middle line:84%
about a game and which
strategies someone might play.

01:12:18.390 --> 01:12:20.370 align:middle line:84%
I think it would be
very natural to say,

01:12:20.370 --> 01:12:22.530 align:middle line:84%
it's reasonable to
play anything that's

01:12:22.530 --> 01:12:25.210 align:middle line:90%
a best response to some belief.

01:12:25.210 --> 01:12:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And it would also be natural to
say, here's what you should do.

01:12:28.470 --> 01:12:30.450 align:middle line:84%
Just don't play
anything that's strictly

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:33.070 align:middle line:90%
dominated by something else.

01:12:33.070 --> 01:12:35.320 align:middle line:84%
And if those two approaches
gave us different answers,

01:12:35.320 --> 01:12:37.070 align:middle line:84%
we'd have to really
think hard about which

01:12:37.070 --> 01:12:38.630 align:middle line:90%
one was more reasonable.

01:12:38.630 --> 01:12:41.230 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out these
are literally going

01:12:41.230 --> 01:12:42.830 align:middle line:90%
to give us the same answer.

01:12:42.830 --> 01:12:44.950 align:middle line:84%
So you can think
positively about what's

01:12:44.950 --> 01:12:47.230 align:middle line:84%
the best response
to something or you

01:12:47.230 --> 01:12:49.430 align:middle line:84%
can think negatively
about what's strictly

01:12:49.430 --> 01:12:51.190 align:middle line:90%
dominated by something.

01:12:51.190 --> 01:12:54.030 align:middle line:84%
And either way, you're
going to get the same two

01:12:54.030 --> 01:12:56.530 align:middle line:84%
groups of strategies-- the
good ones and the bad ones.

01:12:56.530 --> 01:12:59.110 align:middle line:90%


01:12:59.110 --> 01:13:02.510 align:middle line:84%
Let me say a little bit
about how we prove this.

01:13:02.510 --> 01:13:06.810 align:middle line:84%
The proof won't be
covered on exams.

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:08.850 align:middle line:84%
But I want to give
you some idea.

01:13:08.850 --> 01:13:13.550 align:middle line:90%


01:13:13.550 --> 01:13:15.790 align:middle line:90%
So what's the argument?

01:13:15.790 --> 01:13:17.310 align:middle line:90%
Proof?

01:13:17.310 --> 01:13:20.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, how do you show that
exactly one thing happens?

01:13:20.350 --> 01:13:23.650 align:middle line:84%
You have to show that both can't
happen and neither can't happen.

01:13:23.650 --> 01:13:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So another way of saying
it is at most one--

01:13:26.210 --> 01:13:29.656 align:middle line:90%


01:13:29.656 --> 01:13:32.690 align:middle line:84%
so at most one of these
two things can be true

01:13:32.690 --> 01:13:33.670 align:middle line:90%
and at least one.

01:13:33.670 --> 01:13:38.570 align:middle line:90%


01:13:38.570 --> 01:13:40.250 align:middle line:84%
So to say at most
one of the statements

01:13:40.250 --> 01:13:45.250 align:middle line:84%
is true is another way of saying
both statements can't be true--

01:13:45.250 --> 01:13:48.330 align:middle line:90%
at most one, not two.

01:13:48.330 --> 01:13:50.890 align:middle line:84%
At least one is a
way of saying it

01:13:50.890 --> 01:13:53.290 align:middle line:84%
can't be the case
that neither is true,

01:13:53.290 --> 01:13:56.490 align:middle line:84%
at least one, either one
or two, but not zero.

01:13:56.490 --> 01:13:59.090 align:middle line:84%
And by one or two, I
mean the number-- maybe

01:13:59.090 --> 01:14:01.030 align:middle line:84%
I'll call these A and
B to avoid confusion.

01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:03.730 align:middle line:90%


01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:05.865 align:middle line:84%
I'm talking about
at most one, meaning

01:14:05.865 --> 01:14:07.490 align:middle line:84%
the number of statements
that are true,

01:14:07.490 --> 01:14:08.990 align:middle line:84%
not the labels of
the statements.

01:14:08.990 --> 01:14:11.770 align:middle line:90%


01:14:11.770 --> 01:14:15.250 align:middle line:84%
So one of these directions
is a bit easier.

01:14:15.250 --> 01:14:17.097 align:middle line:90%
One of these is quite tricky.

01:14:17.097 --> 01:14:19.430 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone see one that they
think is kind of intuitive

01:14:19.430 --> 01:14:20.478 align:middle line:90%
and they could maybe--

01:14:20.478 --> 01:14:22.770 align:middle line:84%
I won't ask you for the proof,
but could reason through

01:14:22.770 --> 01:14:23.350 align:middle line:90%
on their own?

01:14:23.350 --> 01:14:26.610 align:middle line:90%


01:14:26.610 --> 01:14:27.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:14:27.550 --> 01:14:27.850 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: At most one.

01:14:27.850 --> 01:14:28.767 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: At most one.

01:14:28.767 --> 01:14:31.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is the easier
direction-- not easy

01:14:31.190 --> 01:14:32.550 align:middle line:90%
maybe, but easier.

01:14:32.550 --> 01:14:34.630 align:middle line:84%
And what's your
intuitive argument?

01:14:34.630 --> 01:14:36.920 align:middle line:90%
We won't give a formal one.

01:14:36.920 --> 01:14:38.270 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:14:38.270 --> 01:14:39.855 align:middle line:90%


01:14:39.855 --> 01:14:41.730 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: We're going
to get a contradiction.

01:14:41.730 --> 01:14:44.070 align:middle line:84%
So if both were
true, you're saying

01:14:44.070 --> 01:14:46.028 align:middle line:84%
this is the best we can
do against some belief.

01:14:46.028 --> 01:14:48.570 align:middle line:84%
But there's something else that
does strictly better whatever

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:49.430 align:middle line:90%
my opponent does.

01:14:49.430 --> 01:14:50.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, if it does
strictly better,

01:14:50.490 --> 01:14:52.698 align:middle line:84%
whatever my opponent does,
it should also do strictly

01:14:52.698 --> 01:14:54.110 align:middle line:90%
better given my belief.

01:14:54.110 --> 01:14:56.755 align:middle line:84%
But that means the other thing
couldn't be a best response.

01:14:56.755 --> 01:14:58.130 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's
pretty intuitive.

01:14:58.130 --> 01:15:00.770 align:middle line:84%
I'd encourage you to work
out the algebra on your own.

01:15:00.770 --> 01:15:04.150 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a good
exercise to do at home.

01:15:04.150 --> 01:15:05.650 align:middle line:84%
The at least one
is a lot harder.

01:15:05.650 --> 01:15:07.470 align:middle line:84%
And that I wouldn't
expect you to be

01:15:07.470 --> 01:15:08.690 align:middle line:90%
able to figure out at home.

01:15:08.690 --> 01:15:11.882 align:middle line:84%
There is a proof given in the
appendix of the lecture notes.

01:15:11.882 --> 01:15:13.090 align:middle line:90%
And actually, let me clarify.

01:15:13.090 --> 01:15:14.632 align:middle line:84%
I said I won't test
you on the proof.

01:15:14.632 --> 01:15:16.770 align:middle line:84%
But I could test you on
this part of the proof.

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:18.570 align:middle line:90%
I think the top is fair game.

01:15:18.570 --> 01:15:19.410 align:middle line:90%
The bottom is not.

01:15:19.410 --> 01:15:20.950 align:middle line:90%
Let me be clear about that.

01:15:20.950 --> 01:15:25.910 align:middle line:84%
So the bottom, for the
harder part, see appendix.

01:15:25.910 --> 01:15:28.860 align:middle line:84%
And it uses a
mathematical result

01:15:28.860 --> 01:15:33.460 align:middle line:84%
called either the separating
hyperplane theorem, which

01:15:33.460 --> 01:15:37.780 align:middle line:84%
you may have heard
of, or something

01:15:37.780 --> 01:15:43.100 align:middle line:84%
called Farkas' lemma is also
my preferred way of proving it.

01:15:43.100 --> 01:15:46.740 align:middle line:84%
And just to see why this
is harder, well, if Si--

01:15:46.740 --> 01:15:48.640 align:middle line:84%
we want to show at
least one is true.

01:15:48.640 --> 01:15:51.940 align:middle line:84%
So we have to say if Si is not
a best response to some belief,

01:15:51.940 --> 01:15:53.965 align:middle line:84%
then it's strictly
dominated by something.

01:15:53.965 --> 01:15:55.340 align:middle line:84%
But that means
the proof requires

01:15:55.340 --> 01:15:56.640 align:middle line:90%
you to construct something.

01:15:56.640 --> 01:15:57.660 align:middle line:90%
And that's what's hard.

01:15:57.660 --> 01:16:00.140 align:middle line:84%
All we know is it's not a
best response to any belief.

01:16:00.140 --> 01:16:02.300 align:middle line:84%
But how do we know
what dominates it?

01:16:02.300 --> 01:16:03.920 align:middle line:84%
Well, how do we
come up with that.

01:16:03.920 --> 01:16:04.800 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty hard.

01:16:04.800 --> 01:16:07.220 align:middle line:84%
So this theorem gives
you a way of constructing

01:16:07.220 --> 01:16:10.337 align:middle line:84%
either the belief you need
or the dominating strategy

01:16:10.337 --> 01:16:10.920 align:middle line:90%
that you need.

01:16:10.920 --> 01:16:12.295 align:middle line:90%
And that's much more involved.

01:16:12.295 --> 01:16:13.920 align:middle line:84%
So that won't be
covered in the course.

01:16:13.920 --> 01:16:15.587 align:middle line:84%
But I encourage you
to read the appendix

01:16:15.587 --> 01:16:16.860 align:middle line:90%
if you want to see that.

01:16:16.860 --> 01:16:18.140 align:middle line:90%
And let me stop there.

01:16:18.140 --> 01:16:19.620 align:middle line:90%
And I'll see everyone next week.

01:16:19.620 --> 01:16:22.250 align:middle line:90%
Good luck on the quiz tomorrow.

01:16:22.250 --> 01:16:26.000 align:middle line:90%